2nzR3_uITb2E-UYRj7N_4PxcucQWYx_QaAFI7WhAdtqnhknc-UOydIH0W2Eg8-BzzIm1VDj56kLWmAyMgRHnlaJOWMtvkHYF4HbTeeWNv3AJhP4hwISvMbGge3HSI=\"}"},{"type":"text","text":"I’ll produce an evidence-backed assessment of each: whether the vulnerable code is actually shipped and reachable, what changed upstream, and the smallest compatible remediation. This is investigation only—no dependency changes yet.","textSignature":"{\"v\":1,\"id\":\"msg_0b16ddefcb78f265016ad2faffc2108191a77d74d3a46e7ef4\",\"phase\":\"commentary\"}"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_ce4337d64d5643088571ce883cf19e5b|fc_0b16ddefcb78f265016ad2faffdbd08191accea0059b4ac0a7","name":"read","arguments":{"path":"/Users/alex/.claude/skills/orchestrating-work/SKILL.md","offset":null,"limit":null}},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_cdeaecc4dc9447d9aab33b6a79a65f40|fc_0b16ddefcb78f265016ad2fb008278819190664f630799e1dc","name":"read","arguments":{"path":"/Users/alex/.claude/skills/using-agent-knowledge-base/SKILL.md","offset":null,"limit":null}},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_904d5c42e95b462a9fccaf8aae540f81|fc_0b16ddefcb78f265016ad2fb008278819186cfff219803eda2","name":"mcp","arguments":{"describe":"linear_get_issue"}},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_428109834eb1485d94c377e64feea3a6|fc_0b16ddefcb78f265016ad2fb00827881919f02b8eb6b01ad51","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"git rev-parse --show-toplevel; git status --porcelain=v1; gh api repos/entireio/entire-brain/git/ref/heads/main --jq '{ref,sha:.object.sha}'; entire brain brief 'Deep investigation of COR-2145 setup-go XML-parser advisory and COR-2164 inherited simple-git/argv-parser advisory' --json; entire brain recall 'Prior evidence and unresolved questions for COR-2145 and COR-2164 security assessments' --json","timeout":30}}],"api":"openai-codex-responses","provider":"openai-codex","model":"gpt-6.1-sol","usage":{"input":137,"output":796,"cacheRead":756864,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":516,"totalTokens":757797,"cost":{"input":0.000548,"output":0.011940000000000001,"cacheRead":0.1513728,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0.1638608}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1792211634042,"responseId":"resp_0b16ddefcb78f265016ad2fab430d08191b42653be765a3e79","rawStopReason":"completed","durationMs":78708,"thinkingLevel":"medium"}}
{"type":"message","id":"006eee44","parentId":"d384f590","timestamp":"2026-10-17T04:35:13.403Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_ce4337d64d5643088571ce883cf19e5b|fc_0b16ddefcb78f265016ad2faffdbd08191accea0059b4ac0a7","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"---\nname: orchestrating-work\ndescription: Use for explicit Herdr/delegation requests and automatically for multiple independently ownable outcomes, cross-repository ownership, or clearly long-running visible work.\ncompatibility: Herdr launches require HERDR_ENV=1, herdr CLI, and an existing primary repository workspace for each source repository.\n---\n\n# Orchestrate Work\n\nUse this skill only when its activation policy matches. Otherwise own an answer, investigation, review, or small coherent change directly.\n\n## Activation\n\nActivate immediately for an explicit request to orchestrate, parallelize, delegate high-level work, or use Herdr. Also activate when the request has multiple independently ownable outcomes, cross-repository ownership, or a clearly long-running visible piece.\n\nDo not activate solely because an investigation needs exploration, a task needs a second opinion, or a coherent change needs one bounded helper. A direct session that discovers a trigger loads this skill then.\n\n## Keep parent work shallow\n\nThe parent owns the requested product outcome, priorities, cross-piece contracts, Linear lifecycle, workspace routing, launch/delivery receipts, liveness, merge detection, post-merge verification, cleanup, and final cross-piece conclusion.\n\nExplore only enough to establish repository identity, independently shippable outcomes, dependencies, acceptance evidence, and Linear metadata. Never turn guessed files, symbols, architecture, or implementation steps into delegate requirements. When safe decomposition needs deep repository knowledge, launch one lead workspace agent to return an outcome and dependency map before splitting implementation work.\n\nFor every cross-repository source selection, validate the requested repository's canonical root and primary-workspace identity. UI focus and a guessed path are not evidence.\n\n## Drain to quiescence\n\nAn orchestration turn may settle only when no safe immediate action remains. Apply this on the initial turn and after every watcher, monitor, or subagent notification: a native completion wake resumes orchestration; it is input to the parent loop, not permission to stop with a status summary.\n\nBefore any final or progress response:\n\n1. reconcile the ledger, mission, and delivered events for every incomplete piece;\n2. consume completed outputs and attention requests;\n3. immediately perform every safe available action, including inspecting or nudging a worker, re-arming a watch, launching an unblocked dependency, refreshing required evidence, advancing post-merge verification, or cleaning an eligible workspace; and\n4. repeat until the requested outcome is complete or no safe immediate action remains.\n\nA progress-only response is not a stopping condition. Yield only when every incomplete piece is blocked solely on a recorded external or human signal, or is actively running with its parent-owned watch armed and exact next signal recorded. Name those revisit triggers when yielding. A worker's completed turn is never the parent outcome by itself.\n\n## Prepare the dispatch wave\n\nBefore each launch, the parent owns Linear mutations: correct team, project, milestone, labels, assignee, dependencies, and the transition to In Progress. The workspace agent may read its issue but must not mutate its lifecycle.\n\nBuild this six-field starter packet for every piece:\n\n1. desired outcome and why it matters;\n2. Linear issue ID and authoritative issue requirements;\n3. settled product decisions and hard constraints;\n4. cross-piece interfaces and dependencies;\n5. acceptance signals and granted authority;\n6. known evidence, separated from replaceable hypotheses.\n\nName `owning-workspace-slice` as the required workspace workflow. Do not add unverified repository implementation detail.\n\nInvoke `routing-workspace-agents` once per wave. Record each returned `workspace-agent-route.v1` receipt and pass its selected harness/model to `herdr-launch`. A `needs_extra_usage_decision` receipt is an escalation, never authorization to spend Claude extra usage.\n\nCreate a readable per-run ledger outside tracked repositories under `~/.local/state/herdr/orchestrations/`. When creating it, write this run header before the stream rows:\n\n```text\norchestration_id: <unique run ID>\nobjective: <one-sentence desired outcome>\norchestrator_workspace_id: <HERDR_WORKSPACE_ID>\norchestrator_pane_id: <HERDR_PANE_ID>\nstarted_at: <ISO-8601 timestamp>\n```\n\nThen keep one row per piece:\n\n```text\npiece_id, issue_id, source_workspace_id, workspace_id, checkout_path,\nbranch, harness, model, agent_name, pane_id, prompt_attempts,\nagent_watch_id, armed_at, pr_watch_id, post_merge_watch_id, cleanup_watch_id,\nstate, dependencies, pr, trail, cleanup, outcome\n```\n\nWhen the workspace owner reports its automatically created or linked Trail, record the full canonical `https://entire.io/<forge>/<owner>/<repo>/trails/<number>` URL in that piece's `trail` field immediately. This ledger supplies the recorded workspace ownership tree for the local-only open-Trail status lines in Pi and Claude, including nested controlled workspaces; do not substitute UI focus, `source_workspace_id`, checkout/repository similarity, or a global Trail scan. Do not bulk-evaluate ledger text as shell commands. Legacy ledgers without this header remain valid; never rewrite one merely to support a status report.\n\n### Publish local Trail visibility from existing watches\n\nThe footer never fetches Trail state, polls Entire, or starts a background refresh. When the owner, steward, or existing watch has already observed a Trail's actual `open`, `draft`, `merged`, or `closed` status, publish that fact locally:\n\n```bash\nnode ~/.pi/agent/extensions/session-trail-footer/record-state.mjs \"$trail_url\" \"$trail_status\" \"$observed_at\" --workspace \"$workspace_id\" --pane \"$pane_id\"\n```\n\nUse the Trail owner's verified workspace/pane IDs from its launch receipt, not the relaying orchestrator's IDs. In the owning workspace, omitting the two owner flags uses its actual Herdr caller context. Publication also records that workspace's own Trail association, but never infers a parent edge. Use a canonical URL and the ISO timestamp when the source was observed. A fresh direct observation may omit the timestamp; a relayed receipt must preserve it, never re-stamp stale evidence as current. Publish on initial linking and subsequent status changes observed by the existing workflow, including final Trail closure. Do not make another remote request or create another watch merely to update the footer. A failed lookup leaves local visibility unchanged. PR merge/approval/readiness and ledger cleanup states are not Trail status evidence. Keep ownership rows accurate; a status record alone never creates a parent-child relationship. Existing running agents must pick up this publication step at their next normal wake.\n\n## Launch and arm the workspace watch\n\nUse `herdr-launch` for every worktree launch. It alone validates the source root, creates the worktree, waits for readiness, delivers the starter packet, and performs the one permitted retry.\n\nIn Pi, activate the explicit orchestration guard before the first launch by calling `herdr_agent_monitor` with action `begin` and the absolute ledger path. The guard validates the pane-bound ledger and observes each successful local worktree creation. It blocks a later `herdr worktree create` until the prior created workspace has a matching valid watch receipt in `agent_watch_id` and `armed_at`. End the guard only after its final pending creation has a valid receipt.\n\nFor every `working` receipt, before any next launch:\n\n1. record the `workspace-agent-route.v1` and `herdr-launch` receipts, including source, route, prompt-attempt, and ownership data; ensure the launch's explicit caller-to-child control edge was recorded by `record-workspace.mjs` (repair a missing record from this orchestrator pane using the verified child `workspace_id` and `pane_id`, never the repository source as parent);\n2. create or update the persistent ledger row as `active`;\n3. arm the parent-owned harness watch and record its `agent_watch_id` and `armed_at`; then\n4. enter normal monitoring.\n\nDispatch is not complete until that watch receipt exists. If watch creation fails, preserve the already-running workspace, record the exact failure, stop launching additional pieces, and escalate. `blocked` and `failed` launch receipts escalate immediately and never enter monitoring.\n\n### Non-blocking prompt delivery\n\nUnder this skill, all `herdr agent prompt` delivery omits `--wait`: initial launcher delivery and its permitted retry, parent nudges, resumed work, steward reminders, and cleanup wake-ups. A delivery command is only a receipt that input reached the agent; it never settles agent work:\n\n```bash\nherdr agent prompt \"$agent_name\" \"$message\"\n```\n\nNever wrap that command in a synchronous or foreground Pi tool call, and do not attach a completion timeout to it. The parent must not turn an external Herdr workspace into a synchronous Pi tool call. Settlement belongs exclusively to the existing or newly armed harness-native watcher. After every nonterminal prompt, ensure the watcher is armed or re-armed before yielding; do not wait in the parent for the agent to become idle, done, or blocked.\n\n### Agent watch\n\nA watch observes liveness, dependency changes, completion, merge detection, and cleanup state. It is parent-owned, not process-existence polling or an agent self-watcher. It records each event in the ledger and re-arms while the workspace is active.\n\n- **Claude:** call `Monitor` with `description`, `command`, `persistent: true`, and bounded `timeout_ms`. Its command is exactly one event-driven `herdr agent wait \"$agent_name\" --until blocked --until done --until idle --timeout <bounded-ms>`; never use a polling loop. Record the task ID from `Monitor started (task <task-id>, persistent ...)` as `agent_watch_id`. On event or expected timeout, inspect the complete evidence below and create a fresh Monitor task if still active.\n- **Pi:** call `herdr_agent_monitor` with action `add`, the verified `agent_name`, and a bounded `timeoutMs`. Record its returned monitor ID and `armed_at` as `agent_watch_id` and `armed_at` before any next launch. The tool runs exactly one event-driven `herdr agent wait`, retains its bounded result, and wakes the parent natively. Do not create an async Pi subagent merely to run `herdr agent wait`. Do not replace the native monitor with a polling shell.\n\nOn every event or expected timeout, the parent runs `herdr agent get`, `herdr agent explain \"$agent_name\" --json --verbose`, and `herdr agent read \"$agent_name\" --source recent-unwrapped --lines 120`. Treat `blocked` or a visible blocker as `awaiting_input`; active child state labels as `child_active`; `working` with a parked foreground as `background_only`; and `screen_detection_skipped=true` with `idle` or `done` as `needs_inspection`. `idle` and `done` are terminal-looking signals, not completion without output evidence. A failed `agent get` or persistent `unknown` means the agent disappeared: preserve the workspace and its recorded cleanup recipe, then escalate. In Pi, only after the pane evidence proves completion, call `herdr_agent_monitor` with action `ack` and classification `complete`. The tool performs a fresh `get/explain/read`, requires both `state_change_seq` and `completion_seq`, and stores that bounded inspection receipt; an uninspected or incomplete exited monitor continues to block dispatch.\n\nNever re-arm against the unchanged current terminal state. Record `state_change_seq` and `completion_seq` from the inspection. Re-arm only after parent action or evidence of a new active transition, and record the fresh monitor ID. While Herdr lacks revision-keyed waits, a terminal state with `child_active` waits for a complementary `working|blocked` transition rather than another immediate `idle|done` match.\n\nWatch **creation failure** means no receipt was produced: preserve the already-running workspace, record the exact error, block further dispatch, and escalate. A later **runtime failure**—a Claude Monitor task failure or exit without a handled event, or a Pi monitor in `failed`/`stopped` state—has the same preservation, recording, dispatch-blocking, and escalation result; never remove automatically. These rules apply to agent, PR, post-merge, and cleanup watches. An expected timeout handled by inspection and a justified re-arm is not a runtime failure.\n\n## PR and review watch\n\nWhen a workspace reports a PR, record the receipt and transition `active -> review`; PR readiness is not merge confirmation or cleanup authorization. The workspace owner owns its review steward and all detailed review follow-up. The parent never launches a competing steward.\n\nFor human-facing parent reports (not artifact creation), verify a Trail match only from an actual link or association; repository or branch similarity is not evidence. When one or more verified matching Trails exist, show only their full clickable canonical Trail URL(s) and Trail state; do not identify or link the matched PR, including its URL, number, or identity. PR-derived evidence such as mergeability, check status, and unresolved-thread counts may remain in prose without identifying or linking the PR. If the Trail association exists but Trail state/detail is unavailable, show the Trail URL as unknown and do not fall back to the PR. A closed, merged, or unknown matching Trail remains primary; report its state or discrepancy rather than switching artifacts. If no matching Trail exists, show the clickable PR URL with `Trail: pending/not linked`. For multiple verified matching Trails, show all their canonical URLs and no PR. Never guess or manually create a Trail. Keep artifact creation, automatic Trail linking, review mechanics, personal gates, post-merge verification, and structured/internal PR evidence unchanged. Pi and Claude bottom status lines remain Trail-only.\n\nKeep the agent watch and add a parent-owned aggregate PR/CI/trail watch, recording `pr_watch_id`.\n\n- **Claude:** call `Monitor` with `description`, an aggregate readiness-transition `command`, `persistent: true`, and bounded `timeout_ms`. Record the task ID from `Monitor started (task <task-id>, persistent ...)` as `pr_watch_id`; after task exit or an event, refresh aggregate state and create a fresh task as required.\n- **Pi:** use `review_monitor` where applicable and re-arm it on every actionable transition.\n\nThe GitHub check named exactly `Partial Gates` is always an environment/testing artifact. Exclude it from watches and required-check summaries; it is never a wake signal and does not block merge readiness. This exclusion does not apply to `Entire Gates` or any differently named check.\n\nOnly monitors present in `.monitors[]` are configured. An absent monitor is `not configured`, does not block readiness, and does not create `insufficient_evidence`.\n\nThe parent reads only PR existence/current SHA, **current-head mergeability** (`MERGEABLE`, `UNKNOWN`, or `CONFLICTING`) and its next action/signal, required-check summary (excluding `Partial Gates`), Bugbot terminal presence for that SHA, unresolved GitHub-thread count, `entire trail finding list --freshness any` status/open-finding count, main-review receipt (Trail status/head, runner ID/state/outcome/evaluated SHA, Entire gate summary), **monitor receipt** (PR/Trail head, only monitor keys present in `.monitors[]` with values, states, evaluated SHAs, absent standard keys marked `not configured` without blocking or `insufficient_evidence`, per-canonical-classification disposition counts, evidence-recorded status, actionable count, insufficient-evidence count, and next signal), merge status, dependencies, and completed outcomes. Trail findings are authoritative; a green GitHub gate or opened PR alone is insufficient. Never read finding bodies or logs, monitor rationale, per-claim evidence, import repository-level implementation detail, prescribe fixes, or perform fixes.\n\n### Reconcile settlement before review nudges\n\nReconcile `trail-review=missing` against authenticated Trail detail before nudging or retriggering. Select from `.runners[]` by exact `runner_id == \"trail-review\"`; current-passed requires `state == \"evaluated\"`, `outcome == \"passed\"`, and `evaluated_at_sha == head_sha`, with the Trail head matching the current PR head. Record absent/not-configured, running, stale, failed, and current-passed distinctly. An empty auxiliary summary is never main-review evidence. Genuine absence is not a passing required review; report the configuration/evidence gap. If detail is unavailable, name the next authenticated observation rather than starting another review.\n\nCheck Trail status before mergeability: `.status == \"merged\"` may coexist with false `.mergeability.mergeable`; route direct merge confirmation to post-merge verification, not another review. For open Trails, `.mergeability.mergeable` and `.mergeability.gates[]` (checks, findings, base checks, approvals) are corroborating evidence only; Entire Ready-to-merge never substitutes for Alex's personal current-head gates below. When those gates hold, approval-only Entire failure means **automated work complete; human handoff**, not another review run or an automated approval-polling condition. Keep merge detection armed, but do not nudge the steward for approval.\n\nIf reconciled aggregate state contradicts the owner or stalls, nudge the workspace owner with only the discrepancy or counts and tell it to resume its steward. An absent receipt, or a stale or incomplete receipt for a monitor present in `.monitors[]` (including an unresolved insufficient-evidence count) is an aggregate discrepancy: nudge the owner to complete current-head monitor interrogation without fetching or reading rationale. A current-head `UNKNOWN` mergeability requires the owner to await GitHub's mergeability computation for that SHA; `CONFLICTING` requires the owner to resume its steward for the recorded rebase action. The owner/steward handles CI, Bugbot, threads, trail findings, monitor interrogation, verification, pushes, current-SHA Bugbot checks, re-fetched threads, and refreshed trail findings. Re-arm aggregate watching after each transition through merge. Pre-merge readiness requires stable aggregate zero unresolved review threads and zero open trail findings, with Bugbot terminal, current-passed main review, green required CI excluding `Partial Gates`, and `mergeable == \"MERGEABLE\"` on the current SHA, plus a current-head monitor receipt with no evaluating, stale, or failed present monitor (absent keys are `not configured`, do not block, and do not create `insufficient_evidence`), zero actionable claims, zero insufficient-evidence claims, and documented no-action dispositions, before reporting merge-ready. These pre-merge gates do not prevent recognizing an already merged piece by direct fetch.\n\nReport dependency changes and completed outcomes without importing repository-level implementation detail into the parent context. The parent alone makes the final cross-piece conclusion.\n\n## Post-merge verification and cleanup\n\nTrack each workspace through:\n\n```text\nactive -> review -> merge_confirmed -> post_merge_verifying -> cleaning -> removed\n```\n\nMerge readiness is not merge confirmation or cleanup authorization. After direct merge confirmation, record the actual merge commit, target default branch, and observed default-branch head, then enter `post_merge_verifying`. Bind a parent-owned event-driven build/check watch to that merge commit and default branch, not steward self-polling. Record `post_merge_watch_id`: Claude uses `Monitor` with `description`, a commit/branch-bound check-transition `command`, `persistent: true`, and bounded `timeout_ms`; Pi uses an async watcher. On event or expected timeout, directly refresh the commit-bound check summary and re-arm until terminal; never substitute a later default-branch head or the old PR head. If the default branch advances, retain the actual merge commit as the verification target.\n\nRequire an observed build/check set with the complete required-check inventory and terminal successful required checks for that actual merge commit/default branch; ignore `Partial Gates`. An empty check set is not success, nor are queued, running, missing, cancelled, or failed required checks. Record a current merge-commit success receipt: repository, default branch, actual merge SHA, observed branch head, check/run identities and URLs, terminal conclusions, and observation time. No cleanup, workspace removal, Linear Done, or overall piece Done before that receipt.\n\nOn failure, report the failed check and merge SHA, preserve the workspace in `post_merge_verifying`, and escalate to the owner for investigation. An apparently unrelated failure needs owner-held evidence and an explicit escalation to the parent/user, never silent exclusion or cleanup authorization; the next signal is a successful rerun on the same merge commit. Missing checks or watch failure also preserve the workspace and name the next signal/error. The steward remains pre-merge; the parent alone watches and accepts post-merge success.\n\nOnly after the success receipt, transition to `cleaning` and arm a parent-owned cleanup watch. For Claude, call `Monitor` with `description`, a repo-specific cleanup-transition `command`, `persistent: true`, and bounded `timeout_ms`; record the task ID from `Monitor started (task <task-id>, persistent ...)` as `cleanup_watch_id` and create a fresh task after event/expected timeout while cleaning. For Pi, record the async cleanup watcher run ID as `cleanup_watch_id`. It watches the final cleanup receipt through repository cleanup and `herdr worktree remove` success.\n\n1. wake the owner for its final repository-specific cleanup pass and collect its receipt;\n2. verify every recorded resource is stopped or intentionally retained;\n3. if the owner crashed, inspect and run only recorded cleanup commands one at a time;\n4. confirm the checkout is clean and unoccupied;\n5. run `herdr worktree remove --workspace \"$workspace_id\"` without `--force`;\n6. record cleanup and Linear receipts, transition to `removed`, then move the issue to Done.\n\nDirty, locked, occupied, cleanup-watch-failed, or cleanup-failed workspaces remain open and escalate with the exact remaining state. Never invent repository-specific teardown and never automatically force removal.\n"}],"isError":false,"durationMs":72,"timestamp":1792211713403}}
{"type":"message","id":"5d10d977","parentId":"006eee44","timestamp":"2026-10-17T04:35:13.403Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_cdeaecc4dc9447d9aab33b6a79a65f40|fc_0b16ddefcb78f265016ad2fb008278819190664f630799e1dc","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"---\nname: using-agent-knowledge-base\ndescription: Use when explicitly asked to search or check the shared agent knowledge base, or when investigating recurring repository or system behavior that may have durable prior evidence.\n---\n\n# Use the Agent Knowledge Base\n\nThe shared knowledge base is curated, secondary context in Obsidian vault `Vault`, under `agent-knowledge-base`. This skill is **read-only**: never create, edit, move, archive, or delete a note. Use `capturing-agent-knowledge` only when a write is requested or qualifies for automatic capture.\n\nThis is not Entire `recall`: `recall` searches historical sessions and checkpoints; this skill searches curated reusable notes. Use each only for the context it owns.\n\n## Search before relying on a note\n\n1. Search the knowledge base with Obsidian CLI, always targeting the correct vault and path. Start with the strongest applicable retrieval anchors: issue or incident ID, exact error text or symbol, forge-qualified repository, likely title, reusable tags, and likely aliases. For example:\n   ```sh\n   obsidian vault=Vault search query=\"COR-1623\" path=\"agent-knowledge-base\" format=json\n   obsidian vault=Vault search:context query=\"node liveness error\" path=\"agent-knowledge-base\" format=json\n   ```\n   Do not search or operate elsewhere in the vault.\n2. Read the strongest matches. Use `outline`, `links`, `backlinks`, and note `properties` to understand the claim, its navigation, `status`, `confidence`, and `sources`:\n   ```sh\n   obsidian vault=Vault outline path=\"agent-knowledge-base/repositories/entiredb/example.md\" format=json\n   obsidian vault=Vault links path=\"agent-knowledge-base/repositories/entiredb/example.md\"\n   obsidian vault=Vault backlinks path=\"agent-knowledge-base/repositories/entiredb/example.md\" format=json\n   obsidian vault=Vault properties path=\"agent-knowledge-base/repositories/entiredb/example.md\" format=json\n   ```\n3. Follow the strongest source and validate the claim against current authority: repository code and instructions, Linear, Trail, PR, incident, or command evidence. Do not execute a command copied from a note until its current repository instructions and scope are checked.\n\n## Authority and safety\n\n- Current authoritative sources win over the knowledge base. A note is never a task requirement by itself.\n- Report a contradiction, `superseded` status, or `provisional` status/confidence. Do not silently present stale or weak evidence as current fact.\n- Prefer a source link or identifier over raw logs. Never surface credentials, tokens, private keys, customer data, or sensitive command output.\n- If no useful note is found, say so; do not infer that the behavior is undocumented or impossible.\n\n## Return a compact research receipt\n\nFor each relevant note, report its path, concise claim, status/confidence, strongest source, current verification result, and useful related links. Clearly separate verified current facts from historical or provisional note content.\n"}],"isError":false,"durationMs":70,"timestamp":1792211713403}}
{"type":"message","id":"ba0bcb44","parentId":"5d10d977","timestamp":"2026-10-17T04:35:13.403Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_904d5c42e95b462a9fccaf8aae540f81|fc_0b16ddefcb78f265016ad2fb008278819186cfff219803eda2","toolName":"mcp","content":[{"type":"text","text":"linear_get_issue\nServer: linear\n\nRetrieve detailed information about an issue by ID, including attachments, git branch name, and active Triage Intelligence suggestions when the issue is in triage. A nonempty fields selection overrides the include options; request optional fields directly in fields.\n\nParameters:\n  id (string) *required* - Issue ID or identifier (e.g., LIN-123). P-prefixed identifiers (e.g., P-ENG-123) are projects, not issues\n  fields (array) - Fields to include in each result. Explicitly requested fields with no value return null. `id` is always included. Omit or pass an empty array for the default response.\n    items (enum: \"id\", \"uuid\", \"title\", \"description\", \"projectMilestone\", \"priority\", \"estimate\", \"url\", \"gitBranchName\", \"createdAt\", \"updatedAt\", \"archivedAt\", \"completedAt\", \"startedAt\", \"canceledAt\", \"startedTriageAt\", \"triagedAt\", \"dueDate\", \"slaStartedAt\", \"slaMediumRiskAt\", \"slaHighRiskAt\", \"slaBreachesAt\", \"slaType\", \"status\", \"statusType\", \"labels\", \"triageIntel\", \"createdBy\", \"createdById\", \"assignee\", \"assigneeId\", \"delegate\", \"delegateId\", \"project\", \"projectId\", \"parentId\", \"team\", \"teamId\", \"cycleId\", \"attachments\", \"documents\", \"stateHistory\", \"relations\", \"customerNeeds\", \"releases\")\n  includeRelations (boolean) - Include blocking/related/duplicate relations [default: false]\n  includeCustomerNeeds (boolean) - Include associated customer needs [default: false]\n  includeReleases (boolean) - Include associated releases [default: false]"}],"details":{"mode":"describe","tool":{"name":"linear_get_issue","originalName":"get_issue","description":"Retrieve detailed information about an issue by ID, including attachments, git branch name, and active Triage Intelligence suggestions when the issue is in triage. A nonempty fields selection overrides the include options; request optional fields directly in fields.","inputSchema":{"type":"object","properties":{"id":{"type":"string","description":"Issue ID or identifier (e.g., LIN-123). P-prefixed identifiers (e.g., P-ENG-123) are projects, not issues"},"fields":{"description":"Fields to include in each result. Explicitly requested fields with no value return null. `id` is always included. Omit or pass an empty array for the default response.","type":"array","items":{"type":"string","enum":["id","uuid","title","description","projectMilestone","priority","estimate","url","gitBranchName","createdAt","updatedAt","archivedAt","completedAt","startedAt","canceledAt","startedTriageAt","triagedAt","dueDate","slaStartedAt","slaMediumRiskAt","slaHighRiskAt","slaBreachesAt","slaType","status","statusType","labels","triageIntel","createdBy","createdById","assignee","assigneeId","delegate","delegateId","project","projectId","parentId","team","teamId","cycleId","attachments","documents","stateHistory","relations","customerNeeds","releases"]}},"includeRelations":{"default":false,"description":"Include blocking/related/duplicate relations","type":"boolean"},"includeCustomerNeeds":{"default":false,"description":"Include associated customer needs","type":"boolean"},"includeReleases":{"default":false,"description":"Include associated releases","type":"boolean"}},"required":["id"],"$schema":"https://json-schema.org/draft/2020-12/schema","additionalProperties":false}},"server":"linear"},"isError":false,"durationMs":0,"timestamp":1792211713403}}
{"type":"message","id":"e447bb82","parentId":"ba0bcb44","timestamp":"2026-10-17T04:35:13.403Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_428109834eb1485d94c377e64feea3a6|fc_0b16ddefcb78f265016ad2fb00827881919f02b8eb6b01ad51","toolName":"bash","content":[{"type":"text","text":"/Users/alex/.herdr/worktrees/entire-brain/alex-manage-dependencies\n?? .pi/subagents/\n{\"ref\":\"refs/heads/main\",\"sha\":\"c8624c8826e4d2632510b46041a54694a0504650\"}\nUsage:\n  entire [flags]\n  entire [command]\n\nEntire Setup:\n  agent                      Manage agent integrations (add, remove, list)\n  clean                      Clean up Entire session data\n  configure                  Update Entire settings in the current repository\n  disable                    Disable Entire in current repository\n  doctor                     Diagnose and fix session issues\n  enable                     Enable Entire in current repository\n  plugin                     Manage Entire plugins (install, list, upgrade, search, remove)\n  status                     Show Entire status\n\nSessions & Checkpoints:\n  activity                   Show your activity overview\n  checkpoint                 Inspect and search checkpoints\n  dispatch                   Generate a dispatch summarizing recent agent work\n  recap                      Summarize recent checkpoint activity\n  search                     Search checkpoints, commits, and sessions using semantic and keyword matching\n  session                    Manage agent sessions tracked by Entire\n\nAccount:\n  auth                       Manage authentication\n  login                      Log in to Entire\n  logout                     Log out of Entire\n\nControl Plane:\n  api                        Make an authenticated request to an Entire API and print the response\n  cluster                    Show the Entire clusters you can place projects and repos on\n  org                        Manage Entire organizations\n  project                    Manage Entire projects\n  repo                       Manage Entire repositories\n\nAdditional Commands:\n  agent-help                 Machine-readable usage for coding agents (always matches the installed CLI)\n  help                       Help about any command\n  labs                       Explore experimental Entire workflows\n  version                    Show build information\n\nFlags:\n      --context name   Act as this saved login name for this command only — including the git and agent processes it spawns — instead of the active context (entire auth contexts lists them)\n\nUse \"entire [command] --help\" for more information about a command.\n\nError: Invalid usage: unknown command \"brain\" for \"entire\"\nUsage:\n  entire [flags]\n  entire [command]\n\nEntire Setup:\n  agent                      Manage agent integrations (add, remove, list)\n  clean                      Clean up Entire session data\n  configure                  Update Entire settings in the current repository\n  disable                    Disable Entire in current repository\n  doctor                     Diagnose and fix session issues\n  enable                     Enable Entire in current repository\n  plugin                     Manage Entire plugins (install, list, upgrade, search, remove)\n  status                     Show Entire status\n\nSessions & Checkpoints:\n  activity                   Show your activity overview\n  checkpoint                 Inspect and search checkpoints\n  dispatch                   Generate a dispatch summarizing recent agent work\n  recap                      Summarize recent checkpoint activity\n  search                     Search checkpoints, commits, and sessions using semantic and keyword matching\n  session                    Manage agent sessions tracked by Entire\n\nAccount:\n  auth                       Manage authentication\n  login                      Log in to Entire\n  logout                     Log out of Entire\n\nControl Plane:\n  api            