ut":226535,"output":4775,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":0,"totalTokens":231310,"cost":{"input":0.45307,"output":0.04775,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0.50082}},"fromHook":false,"systemMessage":{"role":"system","content":"","sections":{"preamble":"You are an expert coding assistant operating inside pi, a coding agent harness. You help users by reading files, executing commands, editing code, and writing new files.","tools":"<tools>\n- read: Read file contents\n- bash: Execute bash commands (ls, grep, find, etc.)\n- edit: Make precise file edits with exact text replacement, including multiple disjoint edits in one call\n- write: Create or overwrite files\n- mcp: MCP gateway - connect to MCP servers and call their tools\n- subagents_enable: pi-subagents is installed. For authorized specialist, independent-review, or parallel work, call subagents_enable, then subagent. Authorization must come from the current request or applicable instructions; complexity alone is not authorization.\n- web_enable: If tools for web search, source checking, content fetching, stored-result retrieval are not already available, call web_enable first whenever current, external, or linked information could help; the tools appear on the next model request.\n- web_search: Use for web research questions. Prefer {queries:[...]} with 2-4 varied angles over a single query for broader coverage. Omit provider unless explicitly overriding the configured default.\n- source_check: Gather structured source evidence and passage-level citations for manual semantic review of a claim.\n- fetch_content: Use to fetch URL content, direct images, GitHub repos, and videos.\n- get_search_content: Use after web_search, source_check, or fetch_content to retrieve stored content via responseId. Use findText to locate passages without paging through the full content.\n\nIn addition to the tools above, you may have access to other custom tools depending on the project.\n</tools>","rules":"<rules>\n- Use bash for file operations like ls, rg, find\n- Use read to examine files instead of cat or sed.\n- You can inspect PI_* environment variables for current model and session details.\n- Use edit for precise changes (edits[].oldText must match exactly)\n- When changing multiple separate locations in one file, use one edit call with multiple entries in edits[] instead of multiple edit calls\n- Each edits[].oldText is matched against the original file, not after earlier edits are applied. Do not emit overlapping or nested edits. Merge nearby changes into one edit.\n- Keep edits[].oldText as small as possible while still being unique in the file. Do not pad with large unchanged regions.\n- Use write only for new files or complete rewrites.\n- Be concise in your responses\n- Show file paths clearly when working with files\n</rules>","docs":"<docs>\nPi documentation (read only when the user asks about pi itself, its SDK, extensions, themes, skills, or TUI):\n- Main documentation: /Users/alex/.local/share/mise/installs/node/24.13.0/lib/node_modules/@earendil-works/pi-coding-agent/README.md\n- Additional docs: /Users/alex/.local/share/mise/installs/node/24.13.0/lib/node_modules/@earendil-works/pi-coding-agent/docs\n- Examples: /Users/alex/.local/share/mise/installs/node/24.13.0/lib/node_modules/@earendil-works/pi-coding-agent/examples (extensions, custom tools, SDK)\n- When reading pi docs or examples, resolve docs/... under Additional docs and examples/... under Examples, not the current working directory\n- When asked about: extensions (docs/extensions.md, examples/extensions/), themes (docs/themes.md), skills (docs/skills.md), prompt templates (docs/prompt-templates.md), TUI components (docs/tui.md), keybindings (docs/keybindings.md), SDK integrations (docs/sdk.md), custom providers (docs/custom-provider.md), adding models (docs/models.md), pi packages (docs/packages.md), environment variables (docs/environment-variables.md), MCP servers (docs/mcp.md), codemode scripts and non-LLM models such as classifiers and image models (docs/codemode.md)\n- When working on pi topics, read the docs and examples, and follow .md cross-references before implementing\n- Always read pi .md files completely and follow links to related docs (e.g., tui.md for TUI API details)\n</docs>","project_context":"<project_context>\nProject-specific instructions and guidelines:\n\n<project_instructions path=\"/Users/alex/.pi/agent/AGENTS.md\">\n# Pi global instructions\n\n## Development philosophy\n\n- Default to the simplest thing that works. Name the dumbest viable version first; if not using it, explain why in one line. Prefer few moving parts and one idea per piece over cleverness. Add guards, layers, and abstractions for observed failures, not imagined ones. Simplify mechanism, never verification.\n- Spend care where decisions are hard to undo: persisted schemas, wire formats, and contracts consumed by other teams or repositories. Make those decisions deliberately and at the last responsible moment. For reversible internal structure, build two or three real cases and extract what they share.\n- Friction is data. A third special case, a parameter threaded through many layers, elaborate test setup, a subagent reporting that the design is wrong, or review findings that do not converge are stop signals. Reshape the work instead of brute-forcing it.\n- Be a thinking partner, not a yes-machine. Push back when assumptions or requested mechanics look wrong.\n- For feature and bug-fix implementation, follow the test-driven-development and verification-before-completion skills when available; otherwise apply the same test-first and evidence-before-claims discipline directly. Comments are a last resort; preserve non-obvious intent and invariants, not narration.\n\n## Shared agent knowledge base\n\nThe Obsidian knowledge base is curated secondary context, not authority. Use `using-agent-knowledge-base` for durable prior knowledge and `capturing-agent-knowledge` for explicit or clearly qualified automatic capture; those skills own the detailed workflow and write rules. During orchestration, the parent may read cross-piece context but must not turn unverified note claims into workspace requirements. Vault-note ownership is separate from repository ownership.\n\n## Harness discipline\n\n- Pi is the active agent harness. Use Pi's `subagent` tool for delegation and named-agent execution.\n- Never launch `claude`, `codex`, `copilot`, `gemini`, `opencode`, or another agent CLI as a fallback for a missing Pi agent unless Alex explicitly requests a cross-harness run.\n- When a requested named agent is unavailable, inspect the Pi agent registry and report or adapt the missing agent; do not silently substitute another harness.\n- Custom Pi agents live under `~/.pi/agent/agents/` and execute as Pi child sessions, even when their selected model is from Anthropic or another provider.\n- When the same model is available from multiple providers, prefer the GitHub Copilot-hosted model. Use model-source providers such as `openai-codex` only as fallbacks unless Alex explicitly requests otherwise.\n\n## Work routing\n\nCurated skills that select a model or harness must obtain and honor the shared router receipt. Operator one-shot flags are constraints passed into the router, not permission for a skill to select or substitute a model/harness locally. No skill may locally select, substitute, or reconstruct a receipt, or fall back after router failure. Fixed custom-agent frontmatter and the current top-level session selection remain outside this rule.\n\nOwn normal answers, investigations, reviews, and small coherent changes directly. Name the requested output before starting: an answer, decision, design, or code change. One investigation, second opinion, or bounded helper does not activate orchestration.\n\nLoad `orchestrating-work` for an explicit request to orchestrate, parallelize, delegate high-level work, or use Herdr. Also load it automatically for multiple independently ownable outcomes, cross-repository ownership, or a clearly long-running visible piece. A direct session that discovers one of these triggers loads the skill at that point.\n\nWhen orchestration is active, the parent owns outcomes, dependencies, Linear lifecycle, routing, liveness, merge confirmation, post-merge verification, cleanup, and the final cross-piece conclusion. Keep parent exploration shallow: repository identity, independently shippable outcomes, dependencies, acceptance evidence, and Linear metadata. Do not make unverified files, symbols, architecture, or implementation ideas into workspace requirements. `owning-workspace-slice` owns deep repository exploration, specification, planning, implementation, verification, PR creation, and review follow-up for its slice.\n\nUse workflow skills deliberately rather than assuming discovery: invoke `brainstorming` before creative feature or behavior work; use Pi-native `writing-plans` when a multi-step implementation needs a plan; apply `test-driven-development` for behavior changes and `verification-before-completion` before success claims. Specs may be durable repository artifacts when appropriate. Pi implementation plans are ephemeral, saved outside the tracked tree, and never committed.\n\nIn a Pi workspace slice, `owning-workspace-slice` requires the normal global and project instructions plus `brainstorming`, Pi-native `writing-plans`, the `subagent` tool with configured `pi-subagents` roles, test-driven development, review, and verification. Do not launch another harness to substitute for a missing Pi workflow.\n\nFor an orchestrated Herdr workspace slice, `herdr-launch` is the sole creation path and `orchestrating-work` owns merge-gated cleanup and removal. Do not apply an ordinary local-worktree lifecycle to that Herdr slice.\n\n**Never delegate the conclusion; freely delegate inputs.** Keep reasoning that depends on the conversation in-thread. Delegate bounded discovery, implementation, independent review, and validation when fresh context helps.\n\nTypical Pi role mapping:\n\n- Fast repository reconnaissance and locating files: `scout`.\n- Requirements/context synthesis: `context-builder`.\n- Implementation planning: `planner`.\n- Normal implementation or mechanical edits: `worker` or `delegate`, chosen to match complexity.\n- Independent second opinion or high-context design challenge: `oracle`/`advisor`.\n- Read-only code/diff review: `reviewer`.\n- One bounded PR feedback wave: `review-steward`, one per PR and the only writer in that checkout.\n\nUse one writer per cwd/worktree unless isolated worktrees are intentional. Prefer fresh-context read-only fanout for inputs, then synthesize and apply decisions in the parent. Ordinary children do not orchestrate more children unless explicitly configured for bounded fanout.\n\nAfter delegated work returns, ask four questions:\n\n1. Is the report true? Spot-check a cheap claim from the filesystem, git state, or actual command output.\n2. Is the result good, not merely brief-compliant?\n3. Is the branch over the PR-size ceiling below?\n4. Does the plan still hold given what was learned?\n\nA third attempt at the same problem means the task is probably mis-framed. State what changed since planning and reopen the design rather than dispatching again.\n\nWhen a subagent run creates or discovers a pull request, record it on that run's mission with `subagent` action `mission.update` and an `addReceipts` entry of kind `pull_request`, status `ready`, title `owner/repo#number`, and the PR URL. This keeps the current root session's open PRs visible across repositories and worktrees.\n\n## Worktrees and Entire sessions\n\n- If the cwd is inside a `.worktrees/` path, stay within that worktree for repository operations unless there is a specific reason to leave it.\n- Prefer the current worktree over creating another, but ensure the branch is appropriate and not a shared main checkout.\n- Before writing in a worktree you did not start in, run `entire session current`. Another session bound there means another writer owns it even if the branch looks idle. Never steal the binding with `entire session attach`.\n- For cross-repository work, use the `session-crosslink` skill after committing and before pushing. Attach the session to the worktree holding each affected repository's commits.\n\n## PR sizing\n\nOne PR is one coherent slice. Soft ceiling: roughly 400 net changed lines or 10 files. Hard stop: 800 net changed lines, excluding generated files. Measure with:\n\n```bash\ngit diff --numstat origin/main...HEAD\n```\n\nAt plan time, partition multi-task work into named slices. Each slice needs a one-line explanation of why it ships independently. If a slice exceeds the hard ceiling, split it before dispatching implementation.\n\nAfter each implementation wave, measure again:\n\n- Under soft ceiling: continue if the next task belongs to the same coherent slice.\n- Over soft but independently mergeable: verify and ship now, then start the next slice from main.\n- Over soft and not mergeable: do only the smallest work needed to make the slice coherent.\n- Over hard: stop new implementation until the work is split or a PR is out.\n\nBranch later slices from main, not from an earlier slice, unless the later work genuinely cannot build independently; document intentional stack order.\n\n## PR and review workflow\n\nBefore pushing or creating/updating a PR:\n\n1. Measure PR size.\n2. Run the repository's complete format, lint/static-analysis, and test/build commands—the same wrappers CI uses.\n3. Bring the branch up to date with the default branch.\n4. If the branch has no open PR, run `/preflight-pr` immediately before initial PR creation and before any push performed as part of that creation. Do not trigger it for ordinary pre-PR branch pushes. This is the single local independent-review fanout for that PR; an existing PR never triggers it automatically. After major rework, rerun it only when explicitly requested with `/preflight-pr`.\n5. Inspect the complete diff and working-tree state.\n\nThe GitHub check named exactly `Partial Gates` is always an environment/testing artifact. Ignore it unconditionally in direct and orchestrated review: never inspect, wait on, fix, monitor, or count it as required, and never let it block merge readiness. This does not apply to `Entire Gates` or any differently named check.\n\n### Direct Pi PRs\n\nFor direct, non-orchestrated PR or trail work, default to one Pi `review-steward` per PR. Pass the PR number and absolute worktree path. While it owns that checkout, do not write there from the parent or another worker.\n\nThe parent owns liveness. Use parent-owned `review_monitor` for PR/Trail settlement and merge/build evidence where applicable; record its monitor ID and retain it through human handoff. A steward performs one bounded wave and returns the exact external signal it needs next. Use Pi async `status` and `steer` controls for a live steward; use `resume` only to revive a completed or failed persisted run. Do not make the child poll forever. A completed child turn is not proof that the PR is merge-ready—its result text and direct fetches are authoritative.\n\nReconcile `trail-review=missing` against authenticated Trail detail before nudging or retriggering. Select `.runners[]` by exact `runner_id == \"trail-review\"`; `state == \"evaluated\"`, `outcome == \"passed\"`, and `evaluated_at_sha == head_sha`, with Trail and current PR heads matching, establish current-passed main review. Distinguish `not_configured`, `absent`, `running`, `stale`, `failed`, `current_passed`, and `source_unavailable` in `review_monitor` evidence. A present authenticated runner wins over stale/unknown base configuration; only genuine absence consults configuration. An empty auxiliary summary proves nothing about Main review; unknown auxiliary configuration still prevents full automated settlement. Genuine absence is not a passing required review, and unavailable detail names the next authenticated observation, not another review run.\n\nCheck Trail status before mergeability: `.status == \"merged\"` can coexist with false `.mergeability.mergeable`; directly confirm the actual PR merge and continue to post-merge verification, not another review. For open Trails, `.mergeability.mergeable` and `.mergeability.gates[]` are corroborating evidence only and never substitute for Alex's personal current-head gates below. A `review_monitor` settled wake is terminal automated evidence, not merge-ready authorization; inspect its outcomes because settlement can include failure. When every personal gate holds, approval-only Entire failure means **automated work complete; human handoff**, not another review run or an automated approval-polling condition. Keep parent merge detection armed, without nudging the steward for approval.\n\nPre-merge readiness means direct evidence on the current head: zero unresolved GitHub review threads, zero open Trail findings, Bugbot terminal and current-passed main review, required CI is green excluding `Partial Gates`, GitHub reports `mergeable == \"MERGEABLE\"` on that same head, and a current-head Trail monitor receipt covers only monitors present in `.monitors[]`, with absent standard keys marked `not configured`, and has zero verified actionable or `insufficient_evidence` claims. The receipt records per-canonical-classification disposition counts and evidence-recorded status; stale or opinion claims require documented `no-action` dispositions. Every monitor present in `.monitors[]` must be terminal and evaluated on the current PR and Trail head; absent keys do not block and do not create `insufficient_evidence`, while an evaluating, stale, or failed present monitor remains a wait condition with its exact next signal. `UNKNOWN` mergeability waits for GitHub's computation for that head; `CONFLICTING` requires the steward's rebase action. Replies alone do not resolve threads. Human approval is the handoff point, not an automated wait condition.\n\nAfter about two cleanly descending review waves, only once the full preceding readiness conjunction holds may the steward settle/dismiss repeated non-bugs and report merge-ready. Tail-capping can never bypass current-passed main review, Bugbot terminal, green required CI excluding `Partial Gates`, zero unresolved threads and zero open Trail findings, current-head GitHub `mergeable == \"MERGEABLE\"`, or a complete current-head monitor receipt with zero actionable and `insufficient_evidence` claims and documented `no-action` dispositions. Any push requires re-verifying the full conjunction. Findings that recur in the same area or do not decrease in severity are design friction; stop and choose rework or a follow-up instead of endlessly patching.\n\n### Orchestrated Herdr slices\n\nThe workspace owner creates the human-authored PR, confirms its automatically created or linked Trail, and owns review-loop liveness. Record the full canonical Trail URL immediately in the owning piece's pane-bound orchestration ledger `trail` field; Pi and Claude status lines display only its open or draft Trails, not PRs. Once started, the Pi `review-steward` is the sole writer in that checkout: it reads detailed CI, Bugbot, thread, Trail findings, and monitors; implements fixes, verifies, commits, pushes, and follows feedback to merge readiness. The owner may inspect aggregate state and nudge or resume the steward, but must not edit, commit, or push while the steward owns the checkout. Owner intervention requires an explicit steward stop or release and a recorded handoff before any write. If automatic Trail linking is absent, report the integration problem; do not create a replacement Trail manually.\n\nThe top-level parent owns aggregate readiness, liveness, and post-merge verification: PR/current SHA, current-head mergeability (`MERGEABLE`, `UNKNOWN`, or `CONFLICTING`) and its next action/signal, required-check summary, Bugbot status for that SHA, unresolved-thread count, Trail/open-finding count from `entire trail finding list --freshness any`, main-review receipt (Trail status/head, runner ID/state/outcome/evaluated SHA, Entire gate summary), monitor receipt (PR/Trail head, only monitor keys present in `.monitors[]` with values, states, evaluated SHAs, absent standard keys `not configured` without blocking or `insufficient_evidence`, per-canonical-classification disposition counts, evidence-recorded status, actionable count, insufficient-evidence count, and next signal), merge status, dependencies, and outcomes. It never reads detailed findings, logs, monitor rationale, or per-claim evidence, imports repository-level detail, prescribes fixes, or launches a competing steward. If aggregate state stalls, contradicts the owner, has current-head `UNKNOWN`/`CONFLICTING` mergeability, or has no monitor receipt or a stale/incomplete receipt for a monitor present in `.monitors[]`, nudge the owner with only aggregate discrepancies and tell it to resume its steward. Stable aggregate zero unresolved threads and zero open Trail findings, with Bugbot terminal, current-passed main review, green required CI excluding `Partial Gates`, and `mergeable == \"MERGEABLE\"` on the current SHA and a current monitor receipt with no evaluating, stale, or failed present monitor (absent keys are `not configured`, do not block, and do not create `insufficient_evidence`), zero actionable claims, zero insufficient-evidence claims, and documented `no-action` dispositions, is required for pre-merge readiness, not for recognizing an already merged piece by direct fetch.\n\n## Post-merge completion gate\n\nThe parent owns `review -> merge_confirmed -> post_merge_verifying -> cleaning -> removed`, for direct PRs as well as orchestrated slices. The steward remains pre-merge. After direct merge confirmation, bind the parent-owned watch to the actual merge commit and repository default branch, never the PR head or a later default-branch head. Record the observed default-branch head without retargeting when it advances. `review_monitor` continues polling after review settlement and automatically enters its post-merge phase on GitHub merge confirmation; record that existing monitor ID as `post_merge_watch_id`, not a competing steward or redundant watch. Use Pi `subagent` async watcher semantics only where the tool is inapplicable; record its run ID and handle native completion/failure explicitly. Never substitute another agent CLI or a sleep loop for a missing watcher.\n\nRequire an observed build/check set with the complete required-check inventory and terminal successful required checks for that actual merge commit/default branch. Ignore the exact `Partial Gates` unconditionally; do not treat Cursor Bugbot or Entire Gates review rollups as post-merge build activity. Empty, missing, pending, failed, or cancelled required checks are not success. The tool records `required_names` only when both protection and rules sources are available; `fallback_all_observed` retains every known requirement and requires every observed ordinary check. Its success receipt remains evidence only: the parent must establish the complete required-check inventory before accepting completion, not infer it from fallback or an empty set.\n\nRecord a current merge-commit success receipt: repository, default branch, actual merge SHA, observed branch head, check/run identities and URLs, terminal conclusions, observation time, and required-check selection/inventory evidence. Supplement the tool's bounded summary with direct aggregate GitHub observations for receipt fields it does not supply; do not claim identities, URLs, branch-head observations, or inventory from inference. **No cleanup, workspace removal, Linear Done, or overall piece Done before that receipt.** Only then authorize the owner's repository cleanup, collect its cleanup receipt, verify resources and a clean/unoccupied checkout, and let `orchestrating-work` perform parent-owned removal. Only after cleanup and removal receipts may the parent mark Linear/overall piece Done.\n\nOn failure, no-start, timeout, or watcher error, preserve the workspace in `post_merge_verifying`, report the check/merge SHA and exact next signal/error, and escalate to the owner and parent/user. An apparently unrelated failure needs owner-held evidence and explicit escalation, never silent exclusion or cleanup authorization. The next success must be a successful rerun on the same merge commit. `review_monitor` emits a receipt and stops after a terminal post-merge result; disappearance alone is not success. Retain the receipt, and explicitly establish/re-arm a parent-owned same-commit watch after investigation when needed; do not resume the pre-merge steward as the post-merge watcher.\n\n## Tool use\n\n### File links\n\n- Render source-file references as Markdown links targeting `vscode://file/<absolute-path>:<line>:<column>`, while displaying the repository-relative `path:line`. URL-encode paths when needed.\n\n### Shell\n\n- Pi `bash` tool calls run Bash. Use Bash-compatible syntax in tool commands.\n- Commands handed to Alex should be Fish-native because the interactive login shell is Fish: use `set -x FOO bar`, `for ...; end`, and `and`/`or` where appropriate.\n\n### Slack\n\nAnything an agent posts to Slack must start with `>>>`, which block-quotes the entire body. Apply this to messages, thread replies, and drafts so readers can immediately distinguish agent-authored text from human-authored text.\n\n### Linear\n\nWhen creating issues, set the correct team, project, milestone, and labels. Ask if any are ambiguous.\n\n### Trails and PRs\n\nTrails capture intent and may exist without PRs. Follow the `trail` skill and always give Alex the full clickable Trail URL.\n\nFor human-facing reporting only (not artifact creation), a matched Trail is the artifact reference: show its canonical clickable Trail URL and freshest verified Trail state, and do not identify its linked PR by URL, number, or other identity. When more than one verified Trail matches, show every matching canonical Trail URL and no PR identity. PR-derived readiness evidence may remain in prose without identifying the PR. Unknown matched-Trail details remain Trail-primary: report the known canonical Trail URL and unknown state, never fall back to PR identity. Only when no matching Trail exists may a report show the PR URL with `Trail: pending/not linked`; matching requires an actual link/association, never repository/branch similarity, and no Trail is manually created to fill a gap. Keep the existing artifact-creation policy unchanged. Pi and Claude bottom status lines remain Trail-only.\n\n### Go\n\nAfter a child edits Go files, do not rely on asynchronous gopls diagnostics. Run the repository's actual format, lint, build, and test commands.\n\n</project_instructions>\n\n<project_instructions path=\"/Users/alex/.herdr/worktrees/entire-brain/alex-manage-dependencies/AGENTS.md\">\n<!-- entire-agent:begin -->\nRead .entire/agent-guide.md for this repository's workflow, source inspection, and verification guidance.\n@.entire/agent-guide.md\n<!-- entire-agent:end -->\n\n</project_instructions>\n</project_context>","skills":"<skills>\nThe following skills provide specialized instructions for specific tasks.\nUse the read tool to load a skill's file when the task matches its description.\nWhen a skill file references a relative path, resolve it against the skill directory (parent of SKILL.md / dirname of the path) and use that absolute path in tool commands.\n\n<available_skills>\n  <skill>\n    <name>computer-use</name>\n    <description>Use Orca&apos;s computer-use CLI to inspect and operate local desktop app windows through accessibility trees, screenshots, and safe UI actions. Use for desktop app interaction: list apps/windows, get app state, read visible UI, click controls, type, press keys, scroll, drag, set values, or perform accessibility actions. Also use for browser windows, webviews, Orca app UI, or other desktop UI. Triggers include &quot;computer use&quot;, &quot;orca computer&quot;, &quot;read Spotify&quot;, &quot;read Slack&quot;, &quot;control/click/read in a desktop app&quot;, and &quot;get app state&quot;.</description>\n    <location>/Users/alex/.pi/agent/skills/computer-use/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>entire-graph</name>\n    <description>Use FIRST to locate code for any task in a git repo — &quot;where do I fix X&quot;, &quot;who calls X&quot;, &quot;what breaks if I change this&quot;, blast radius, what a type/interface is and who implements it, entity-level review of a commit/branch/checkpoint. Runs the local `entire graph` code graph (deterministic, no network, no LLM) instead of broad grep-and-read. Not for lexical find of a known literal string (use peregrine/grep) or for reading patch text (use git).</description>\n    <location>/Users/alex/.pi/agent/skills/entire-graph/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>find-skills</name>\n    <description>Helps users discover and install agent skills when they ask questions like &quot;how do I do X&quot;, &quot;find a skill for X&quot;, &quot;is there a skill that can...&quot;, or express interest in extending capabilities. This skill should be used when the user is looking for functionality that might exist as an installable skill.</description>\n    <location>/Users/alex/.pi/agent/skills/find-skills/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>orca-cli</name>\n    <description>Use the public `orca` CLI to operate Orca-managed worktrees, folder contexts, terminals, repos, automations, artifacts, skill sharing, worktree comments, and the browser embedded inside the Orca app. Use when the user says &quot;$orca-cli&quot;, &quot;use orca cli&quot;, &quot;Orca worktree&quot;, &quot;child worktree&quot;, &quot;cardStatus&quot;, &quot;spawn codex/claude in a worktree&quot;, &quot;read/wait/send Orca terminal&quot;, &quot;terminal send&quot;, &quot;full handoff&quot;, &quot;handover&quot;, &quot;give this to another agent&quot;, &quot;another worktree&quot;, &quot;Orca browser&quot;, &quot;orca artifacts&quot;, &quot;share HTML/Markdown&quot;, &quot;public artifact link&quot;, &quot;share skills&quot;, or &quot;control the browser inside Orca&quot;. Prefer this over raw `git worktree`, ad hoc PTYs, Playwright, or Computer Use when the task touches Orca-managed state. Use Computer Use for browser windows, webviews, or desktop UI outside Orca&apos;s embedded browser.</description>\n    <location>/Users/alex/.pi/agent/skills/orca-cli/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>orchestration</name>\n    <description>Use Orca orchestration for structured multi-agent coordination: threaded messages, blocking ask/reply flows, task dispatch, worker_done/escalation waits, task DAGs, decision gates, coordinator loops, or decomposing work across agents. Use `orca-cli` instead for full ownership handoffs, including requests phrased as &quot;hand off&quot;, &quot;handoff&quot;, &quot;handover&quot;, &quot;give this to another agent&quot;, or &quot;another worktree&quot; when the user did not explicitly ask to supervise, monitor, wait for results, or coordinate a DAG. Use `orca-cli` for ordinary terminal control, lightweight terminal prompts, shell commands, Orca worktree management, reading or waiting on terminals, and automation of the browser embedded inside Orca. Use Computer Use for browser windows, webviews, Orca app UI, or desktop UI outside Orca&apos;s embedded browser.</description>\n    <location>/Users/alex/.pi/agent/skills/orchestration/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>writing-plans</name>\n    <description>Use when requirements or a spec need a concrete multi-step implementation plan before changing code.</description>\n    <location>/Users/alex/.pi/agent/skills/writing-plans/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>address-findings</name>\n    <description>Address the review findings posted on an Entire trail: fetch the trail&apos;s open findings, fix the code, and resolve them on the trail. Use when the user gives a trail URL or number and asks to address, fix, or resolve its findings or review comments. Not for local diff review — that is the `review` skill.\n</description>\n    <location>/Users/alex/.agents/skills/entire/skills/address-findings/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>explain</name>\n    <description>Explains the intent behind source code by finding original session transcripts. Use explain with a function, file, or line of code to understand why it exists.</description>\n    <location>/Users/alex/.agents/skills/entire/skills/explain/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>recall</name>\n    <description>Use when the user describes a task and wants to know whether something similar has been done before, then turn the closest prior session into a task playbook. Triggers on phrases like &quot;have we done this before&quot;, &quot;recall how we did X&quot;, &quot;find similar work&quot;, &quot;any precedent for&quot;, &quot;has anyone solved&quot;, &quot;is there a template for&quot;, and &quot;how did we do this last time&quot;</description>\n    <location>/Users/alex/.agents/skills/entire/skills/recall/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>replay</name>\n    <description>Use when the user wants to step through a feature&apos;s checkpoints chronologically, pausing at each step to ask questions. Triggers on phrases like &quot;replay &lt;feature&gt;&quot;, &quot;walk me through how X was built&quot;, &quot;show me the journey of&quot;, &quot;step me through how Y was implemented&quot;, and &quot;replay the last week&quot;</description>\n    <location>/Users/alex/.agents/skills/entire/skills/replay/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>review</name>\n    <description>Review code changes on the current branch using checkpoint transcript context to understand developer intent before auditing the diff. Use when the user asks for a code review, wants to review branch changes, or asks to audit recent work before merging.\n</description>\n    <location>/Users/alex/.agents/skills/entire/skills/review/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>search</name>\n    <description>Use when the user wants to find prior work, checkpoints, or agent conversations by topic, repo, branch, author, or recent time window, or to search code content across repositories</description>\n    <location>/Users/alex/.agents/skills/entire/skills/search/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>session-crosslink</name>\n    <description>Use when an agent session ran outside the repo whose commits should record it — e.g. launched from a higher-level folder, a non-Entire repo, or one repo but editing another — to attach the session to each affected Entire-enabled repo&apos;s HEAD commit.</description>\n    <location>/Users/alex/.agents/skills/entire/skills/session-crosslink/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>session-handoff</name>\n    <description>Use when the user wants to continue work from one agent in another agent, inspect recent sessions, or summarize a saved session or checkpoint for handoff</description>\n    <location>/Users/alex/.agents/skills/entire/skills/session-handoff/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>session-to-skill</name>\n    <description>Use when the user wants to turn one or more Entire-tracked sessions, checkpoints, or repeated agent workflows into a reusable agent skill.</description>\n    <location>/Users/alex/.agents/skills/entire/skills/session-to-skill/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>teach</name>\n    <description>Use when a developer wants a topic-focused guided lesson built from canonical checkpoints, not a whole-repo overview. Triggers on phrases like &quot;teach me &lt;topic&gt;&quot;, &quot;teach me how this repo handles&quot;, &quot;how does &lt;topic&gt; work in this repo&quot;, &quot;give me a lesson on&quot;, &quot;school me on&quot;, and &quot;I need to learn about&quot;</description>\n    <location>/Users/alex/.agents/skills/entire/skills/teach/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>using-entire</name>\n    <description>Use for any codebase exploration or understanding task — reads historical intent from Entire checkpoints instead of guessing. Orchestrates other Entire skills to give the agent provenance-backed answers about code.\n</description>\n    <location>/Users/alex/.agents/skills/entire/skills/using-entire/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>what-happened</name>\n    <description>Explain why code looks the way it does by tracing the latest change for a file range or pasted snippet through `git blame` and deduplicated `entire explain` lookups. Use when the user asks what happened, says &quot;tell me why&quot; about a code block, is confused about a section of code, asks &quot;wtf is going on&quot;, &quot;why is this like this&quot;, &quot;why was this changed&quot;, or wants provenance for a specific file block.\n</description>\n    <location>/Users/alex/.agents/skills/entire/skills/what-happened/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>council-mode</name>\n    <description>Run a bounded supervisor-mediated advisor council. Use when the user asks for council mode, asks to convene advisors, debate a decision, cross-examine recommendations, or run /council.</description>\n    <location>/Users/alex/.pi/agent/git/github.com/nicobailon/pi-subagents/skills/council-mode/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>pi-subagents</name>\n    <description>Technical guidance for operator-requested delegation to builtin or custom\nsubagents: bounded handoffs, parallel review, scripted workflows, async work,\nforked context, isolation, and coordinated execution.\n</description>\n    <location>/Users/alex/.pi/agent/git/github.com/nicobailon/pi-subagents/skills/pi-subagents/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>frontend-design</name>\n    <description>Guidance for distinctive, intentional visual design when building new UI or reshaping an existing one. Helps with aesthetic direction, typography, and making choices that don&apos;t read as templated defaults.</description>\n    <location>/Users/alex/.claude/plugins/cache/claude-plugins-official/frontend-design/c2bdb427efe8/skills/frontend-design/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>brainstorming</name>\n    <description>You MUST use this before any creative work - creating features, building components, adding functionality, or modifying behavior. Explores user intent, requirements and design before implementation.</description>\n    <location>/Users/alex/.claude/plugins/cache/claude-plugins-official/superpowers/6.4.1/skills/brainstorming/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>receiving-code-review</name>\n    <description>Use when receiving code review feedback, before implementing suggestions, especially if feedback seems unclear or technically questionable - requires technical rigor and verification, not performative agreement or blind implementation</description>\n    <location>/Users/alex/.claude/plugins/cache/claude-plugins-official/superpowers/6.4.1/skills/receiving-code-review/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>systematic-debugging</name>\n    <description>Use when encountering any bug, test failure, or unexpected behavior, before proposing fixes</description>\n    <location>/Users/alex/.claude/plugins/cache/claude-plugins-official/superpowers/6.4.1/skills/systematic-debugging/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>test-driven-development</name>\n    <description>Use when implementing any feature or bugfix, before writing implementation code</description>\n    <location>/Users/alex/.claude/plugins/cache/claude-plugins-official/superpowers/6.4.1/skills/test-driven-development/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>verification-before-completion</name>\n    <description>Use when about to claim work is complete, fixed, or passing, before committing or creating PRs - requires running verification commands and confirming output before making any success claims; evidence before assertions always</description>\n    <location>/Users/alex/.claude/plugins/cache/claude-plugins-official/superpowers/6.4.1/skills/verification-before-completion/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>writing-skills</name>\n    <description>Use when creating new skills, editing existing skills, or verifying skills work before deployment</description>\n    <location>/Users/alex/.claude/plugins/cache/claude-plugins-official/superpowers/6.4.1/skills/writing-skills/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>capturing-agent-knowledge</name>\n    <description>Use when explicitly asked to record, remember, or document durable agent knowledge, or after discovering verified reusable knowledge that clearly qualifies for automatic capture.</description>\n    <location>/Users/alex/.claude/skills/capturing-agent-knowledge/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>herdr</name>\n    <description>Control Herdr, a terminal multiplexer for coding agents. Use only when the user explicitly mentions Herdr or asks to use Herdr to inspect or control panes, tabs, workspaces, commands, or another agent. Do not use merely because a task could benefit from a background terminal, delegation, or parallel work. Requires HERDR_ENV=1.</description>\n    <location>/Users/alex/.claude/skills/herdr/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>herdr-launch</name>\n    <description>Use for the mechanical launch of a routed Pi or Claude agent in a new Herdr worktree workspace.</description>\n    <location>/Users/alex/.claude/skills/herdr-launch/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>humanizer</name>\n    <description>Remove signs of AI-generated writing from text. Use when editing or reviewing\ntext to make it sound more natural and human-written. Based on Wikipedia&apos;s\ncomprehensive &quot;Signs of AI writing&quot; guide. Detects and fixes patterns including:\ninflated symbolism, promotional language, superficial -ing analyses, vague\nattributions, em dash overuse, rule of three, AI vocabulary words, negative\nparallelisms, and excessive conjunctive phrases.\n</description>\n    <location>/Users/alex/.claude/skills/humanizer/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>interrogating-slopgate</name>\n    <description>Use when the user says &quot;check slopgate&quot;, &quot;there are slopgate findings&quot;, &quot;slopgate is medium/high/large&quot;, mentions slop gate feedback, or asks to inspect the Slop, Risk, Security, or Confidence monitor on an Entire Trail.</description>\n    <location>/Users/alex/.claude/skills/interrogating-slopgate/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>mate</name>\n    <description>Restate the last message in plain human language, with no jargon. Use for &quot;dumb it down&quot;, &quot;explain it simply&quot;, &quot;too much text&quot;, &quot;be brief&quot;, &quot;in short simple terms&quot;, or /mate.</description>\n    <location>/Users/alex/.claude/skills/mate/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>orchestrating-work</name>\n    <description>Use for explicit Herdr/delegation requests and automatically for multiple independently ownable outcomes, cross-repository ownership, or clearly long-running visible work.</description>\n    <location>/Users/alex/.claude/skills/orchestrating-work/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>owning-workspace-slice</name>\n    <description>Use when a Herdr workspace agent owns one coherent repository slice from discovery through a reviewed, merge-ready PR.</description>\n    <location>/Users/alex/.claude/skills/owning-workspace-slice/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>peregrine</name>\n    <description>Use when searching for code, finding definitions, tracing references, locating files, or exploring codebases — prefer peregrine over Grep, Glob, and other file search tools for all code search tasks</description>\n    <location>/Users/alex/.claude/skills/peregrine/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>review-others-trail</name>\n    <description>Privately review somebody else&apos;s Entire Trail from its verified checkout, as a default sanity check or explicitly requested deep review. Use for a Trail URL even without a Herdr launcher or PR; never post or fix as part of review.</description>\n    <location>/Users/alex/.claude/skills/review-others-trail/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>routing-workspace-agents</name>\n    <description>Route curated skill model and harness selections, including workspace-agent dispatch waves, through the shared quota-aware authority.</description>\n    <location>/Users/alex/.claude/skills/routing-workspace-agents/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>trail</name>\n    <description>Use when a discovery cycle has converged on a spec/design doc and work is about to move into implementation, or when the user types /trail or asks to create, view, or update an Entire &quot;trail&quot;. Also use when asked to capture the intent of a piece of work, or to attach a branch&apos;s sessions/checkpoints to a trail. Manages trails via `entire trail`.</description>\n    <location>/Users/alex/.claude/skills/trail/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>using-agent-knowledge-base</name>\n    <description>Use when explicitly asked to search or check the shared agent knowledge base, or when investigating recurring repository or system behavior that may have durable prior evidence.</description>\n    <location>/Users/alex/.claude/skills/using-agent-knowledge-base/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>where-are-we-at</name>\n    <description>Use when the user asks “where are we at,” “catch me up,” requests an orchestrator status update, or returns to an orchestrator pane after context may have scrolled away or changed.</description>\n    <location>/Users/alex/.claude/skills/where-are-we-at/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n</available_skills>\n</skills>","cwd":"<cwd>\n/Users/alex/.herdr/worktrees/entire-brain/alex-manage-dependencies\n</cwd>"},"toolsAdded":[{"name":"read","description":"Read the contents of a file. Supports text files and images (jpg, png, gif, webp, bmp). Images are sent as attachments. For text files, output is truncated to 2000 lines or 50KB (whichever is hit first). Use offset/limit for large files. When you need the full file, continue with offset until complete.","parameters":{"type":"object","required":["path"],"properties":{"path":{"type":"string","description":"Path to the file to read (relative or absolute)"},"offset":{"type":"number","description":"Line number to start reading from (1-indexed)"},"limit":{"type":"number","description":"Maximum number of lines to read"}}},"constrainedSampling":{"type":"json_schema","strict":"prefer"}},{"name":"bash","description":"Execute a bash command in the current working directory. Returns stdout and stderr. Output is truncated to last 2000 lines or 50KB (whichever is hit first). If truncated, full output is saved to a temp file. Optionally provide a timeout in seconds.","parameters":{"type":"object","required":["command"],"properties":{"command":{"type":"string","description":"Shell command to execute"},"timeout":{"type":"number","description":"Timeout in seconds (optional, no default timeout)"}}},"constrainedSampling":{"type":"json_schema","strict":"prefer"}},{"name":"edit","description":"Edit a single file using exact text replacement. Every edits[].oldText must match a unique, non-overlapping region of the original file. If two changes affect the same block or nearby lines, merge them into one edit instead of emitting overlapping edits. Do not include large unchanged regions just to connect distant changes.","parameters":{"type":"object","required":["path","edits"],"properties":{"path":{"type":"string","description":"Path to the file to edit (relative or absolute)"},"edits":{"type":"array","items":{"type":"object","required":["oldText","newText"],"properties":{"oldText":{"type":"string","description":"Exact text for one targeted replacement. It must be unique in the original file and must not overlap with any other edits[].oldText in the same call."},"newText":{"type":"string","description":"Replacement text for this targeted edit."}}},"description":"One or more targeted replacements. Each edit is matched against the original file, not incrementally. Do not include overlapping or nested edits. If two changes touch the same block or nearby lines, merge them into one edit instead."}}},"constrainedSampling":{"type":"json_schema","strict":"prefer"}},{"name":"write","description":"Write content to a file. Creates the file if it doesn't exist, overwrites if it does. Automatically creates parent directories.","parameters":{"type":"object","required":["path","content"],"properties":{"path":{"type":"string","description":"Path to the file to write (relative or absolute)"},"content":{"type":"string","description":"Content to write to the file"}}},"constrainedSampling":{"type":"json_schema","strict":"prefer"}},{"name":"claude_sync_status","description":"Inspect the policy-controlled synchronization status of selected Claude Code resources into Pi without reading or exposing credentials.","parameters":{"type":"object","properties":{}}},{"name":"herdr_agent_monitor","description":"Run one parent-owned event-driven Herdr agent wait, retain its receipt/result, and guard explicitly registered orchestration against unwatched next launches.","parameters":{"type":"object","required":["action"],"properties":{"action":{"type":"string","enum":["begin","add","list","status","ack","stop","end"]},"ledger":{"type":"string"},"agent":{"type":"string"},"timeoutMs":{"type":"integer","minimum":1000,"maximum":86400000},"until":{"type":"array","items":{"type":"string","enum":["idle","working","blocked","done"]},"minItems":1,"uniqueItems":true},"id":{"type":"string"},"classification":{"type":"string","enum":["complete"]}}}},{"name":"review_monitor","description":"Watch automated PR/Trail review settlement, then merged-commit builds. Evidence only; never bypasses personal gates or decides cleanup/Done.","parameters":{"type":"object","required":["action"],"properties":{"action":{"type":"string","enum":["add","list","status","rearm","stop","stop_all"]},"repo":{"type":"string"},"pr":{"type":"integer","minimum":1},"trail":{"type":"string"},"intervalSeconds":{"type":"integer","minimum":15,"maximum":300}}}},{"name":"mcp","description":"MCP gateway - connect to MCP servers and call their tools. Non-MCP Pi tools should be called directly, not through mcp.\n\nServers: daytona (39 tools), slack (27 tools), datadog (169 tools), linear (68 tools), incident_io (119 tools)\n\nServer instructions (truncated - full text via mcp({ instructions: \"name\" })):\n  datadog: This is an MCP server for Datadog (a software observability platform). See Datadog docs if you're unsure about how something works in the platform....\n  linear: When passing string values to tools, send the content directly without escape sequences. For example, use real newlines in markdown content rather...\n\nUsage:\n  mcp({ })                              → Show server status\n  mcp({ server: \"name\" })               → List tools from server\n  mcp({ search: \"query\" })              → Search MCP tools by name/description\n  mcp({ describe: \"tool_name\" })        → Show tool details and parameters\n  mcp({ instructions: \"name\" })         → Show full server usage instructions\n  mcp({ connect: \"server-name\" })       → Connect to a server and refresh metadata\n  mcp({ tool: \"name\", args: { key: \"value\" } })         → Call a tool (object args; JSON string also accepted)\n  mcp({ action: \"ui-messages\" })        → Retrieve accumulated messages from completed UI sessions\n  mcp({ action: \"auth-start\", server: \"name\" })      → Start manual OAuth and get a browser URL\n  mcp({ action: \"auth-complete\", server: \"name\", args: { redirectUrl: \"...\" } }) → Complete manual OAuth\n\nMode: action > tool (call) > connect > describe > instructions > search > server (list) > nothing (status)","parameters":{"type":"object","properties":{"tool":{"type":"string","description":"Tool name to call (e.g., 'xcodebuild_list_sims')"},"args":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string","description":"Arguments as a JSON string (e.g., '{\"key\": \"value\"}')"},{"type":"object","properties":{},"additionalProperties":true,"description":"Arguments as a JSON object (e.g., { \"key\": \"value\" })"}],"description":"Tool arguments as a JSON object, or as a JSON string encoding one"},"connect":{"type":"string","description":"Server name to connect (lazy connect + metadata refresh)"},"describe":{"type":"string","description":"Tool name to describe (shows parameters)"},"instructions":{"type":"string","description":"Server name to show that server's usage instructions"},"search":{"type":"string","description":"Search tools by name/description"},"regex":{"type":"boolean","description":"Treat search as regex (default: substring match)"},"includeSchemas":{"type":"boolean","description":"Include parameter schemas in search results (default: true)"},"server":{"type":"string","description":"Filter to specific server (also disambiguates tool calls)"},"action":{"type":"string","description":"Action: 'ui-messages', 'auth-start', or 'auth-complete'"}}}},{"name":"bg_wait","description":"Wait for background, provider, or detached work that has no native completion notification, then return.\n\nOrdinary async subagent runs already notify this session natively when they complete or need attention. In an interactive chat, return control instead of calling this merely to wait. Use this tool for provider jobs, remembered detached foreground runs, or other background work without a native notification path. Headless runs auto-drain current-session subagent work at agent_end; use this tool only when the current turn must receive non-notifying background work results.\n\n• { } — return when the first initially active async run or registered provider item finishes, or when a subagent needs attention.\n• { all: true } — wait for every async run, provider item, and remembered detached foreground descendant that was active when the call began.\n• { id: \"...\" } — wait for one async or remembered detached foreground subagent run (id or prefix). Named async runs that already finished return their terminal result references.\n• { id: \"...\", nonBlocking: true } — resolve the prefix once, persist an exact-run wake subscription, and return immediately. Use this for detached work without native completion delivery; the originating interactive session wakes on completion, failure, attention, reconciliation failure, or timeout.\n• { stopOnAttention: false } — for blocking waits only, keep waiting through idle or long-thinking attention; supervisor/contact requests still stop the wait.\n• { timeoutMs: 600000 } — stop waiting after N ms; active work keeps running. Omitted values use waitTool.defaultTimeoutMs, then 30 minutes. Window expiry returns a non-error window_elapsed result with active work identities.\n\nNon-blocking subscriptions are visible in subagent status and differ from disabling waitTool: waitTool.enabled=false returns immediately without registering any future wake. Provider jobs are session-scoped and identified exactly, so replacing one job with another cannot hide a completion. Provider extensions must be explicitly loaded in this process. In a child agent, keep `bg_wait` in the child tool allowlist and load each provider through the agent's extensions or subagentOnlyExtensions; this tool never loads providers or grants tools itself.","parameters":{"type":"object","properties":{"id":{"type":"string","description":"Async run or remembered detached foreground run id/prefix to wait for one specific run. Ordinary async subagent runs already notify this session natively; use bg_wait for provider, detached, or other background work without native notification, or when same-turn blocking results are truly needed. Omit to wait across every active async run started in this session only when a same-turn wait is truly needed."},"nonBlocking":{"type":"boolean","description":"When true, resolve id to one exact run, persist a wake subscription, and return immediately. Use this only for provider, detached, or other background work without a native completion notification; ordinary async subagent runs already notify this session natively and do not need a subscription. The originating session is woken on completion, failure, attention, reconciliation failure, or timeout. Requires id and cannot be combined with all."},"all":{"type":"boolean","description":"Wait for ALL active runs to finish. Ordinary async subagent runs already notify this session natively; use all only when a same-turn result from tracked background work is truly needed. Default false: return when the first tracked run or provider item finishes or needs attention. Ignored when id targets a single run."},"timeoutMs":{"type":"integer","minimum":1,"description":"Give up waiting after this many milliseconds (the runs keep going regardless). Ordinary async subagent runs already notify this session natively; use a wait timeout only when same-turn results are truly needed for provider, detached, or other background work without native notification. Defaults to config waitTool.defaultTimeoutMs, then 1800000 (30 minutes). Window expiry is a non-error active-work result."},"stopOnAttention":{"type":"boolean","description":"For a blocking wait that is truly needed, stop when a run needs attention by default. Set false to keep waiting through idle or long-thinking attention; supervisor/contact requests still stop the wait."}}}},{"name":"subagents_enable","description":"Enable pi-subagents delegation and management tools without launching work. Call when delegation is authorized by the current request or applicable user/project instructions, or when managing existing runs. Direct execution is the default; complexity alone never authorizes delegation. Full tools are available on the next model request.","parameters":{"type":"object","properties":{}}},{"name":"web_enable","description":"Enable configured pi-web-access tools for web research and content retrieval. Does not search or fetch. Enabled tools are available on the next model request; disabled capabilities remain unavailable.","parameters":{"type":"object","properties":{},"additionalProperties":false}},{"name":"subagent_supervisor","description":"Native pi-subagents supervisor channel. Use reply/pending/status to answer child subagent requests without overriding pi-intercom.","parameters":{"type":"object","required":["action"],"properties":{"action":{"type":"string","enum":["list","pending","status","reply"]},"to":{"type":"string"},"message":{"type":"string"},"replyTo":{"type":"string"}},"additionalProperties":false}},{"name":"web_search","description":"Search the web with OpenAI, Brave, Parallel, Parallel MCP, TinyFish, Search1API, Searchinfinity, Querit, Tavily, You.com, Firecrawl, Jina, SearXNG, DuckDuckGo, Perplexity, Gemini, Kimi, Exa, SERPdive, Kagi, Ollama, Anysearch, xAI, Mistral, Bright Data, SerpBase, SerpApi, Serper, Serply, Valyu, Bocha, XCrawl, Baizhi. Provider arrays run simultaneously; all searches eligible allowed providers (OpenAI, Brave, Parallel, TinyFish, Search1API, Searchinfinity, Querit, Tavily, Firecrawl, Jina, SearXNG, Perplexity, Gemini, Exa, SERPdive, Kagi, Ollama, Bocha); explicit-only allowed providers (Parallel MCP, You.com, DuckDuckGo, Kimi, Anysearch, xAI, Mistral, Bright Data, SerpBase, SerpApi, Serper, Serply, Valyu, XCrawl, Baizhi) remain excluded. The default workflow is none: it returns bounded source-linked search results or provider answers without a curator or generated summary, identifies the providers used, and stores full results for retrieval by responseId. For comprehensive research, prefer queries (plural) with 2-4 varied angles over a single query. When includeContent is true, full page content is fetched in the background. Set workflow to \"summary-review\" to open the curator with an auto-generated summary draft or \"auto-summary\" to generate a summary without the browser curator. The configured provider is used when provider is omitted or set to auto; omit provider unless explicitly overriding it.","parameters":{"type":"object","properties":{"query":{"type":"string","description":"Single search query. For research tasks, prefer 'queries' with multiple varied angles instead."},"queries":{"type":"array","items":{"type":"string"},"description":"Multiple queries searched concurrently (up to three at a time), each returning source-linked search results or a provider answer. Prefer this for research — vary phrasing, scope, and angle across 2-4 queries to maximize coverage. Good: ['React vs Vue performance benchmarks 2026', 'React vs Vue developer experience comparison', 'React ecosystem size vs Vue ecosystem']. Bad: ['React vs Vue', 'React vs Vue comparison', 'React vs Vue review'] (too similar, redundant results)."},"numResults":{"type":"integer","minimum":1,"maximum":20,"description":"Results per query (default: 5, max: 20)"},"includeContent":{"type":"boolean","description":"Fetch full page content (async)"},"recencyFilter":{"type":"string","enum":["day","week","month","year"],"description":"Filter by recency"},"domainFilter":{"type":"array","items":{"type":"string"},"description":"Limit to domains (prefix with - to exclude)"},"provider":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string","enum":["auto","all","openai","brave","parallel","parallel-mcp","tinyfish","search1api","searchinfinity","querit","tavily","you","firecrawl","jina","searxng","duckduckgo","perplexity","gemini","kimi","exa","serpdive","kagi","ollama","anysearch","xai","mistral","brightdata","serpbase","serpapi","serper","serply","valyu","bocha","xcrawl","baizhi"]},{"type":"array","items":{"type":"string","enum":["openai","brave","parallel","parallel-mcp","tinyfish","search1api","searchinfinity","querit","tavily","you","firecrawl","jina","searxng","duckduckgo","perplexity","gemini","kimi","exa","serpdive","kagi","ollama","anysearch","xai","mistral","brightdata","serpbase","serpapi","serper","serply","valyu","bocha","xcrawl","baizhi"]},"minItems":1}],"description":"Search provider or non-empty list of allowed providers to search simultaneously; all searches eligible allowed providers (OpenAI, Brave, Parallel, TinyFish, Search1API, Searchinfinity, Querit, Tavily, Firecrawl, Jina, SearXNG, Perplexity, Gemini, Exa, SERPdive, Kagi, Ollama, Bocha); explicit-only allowed providers (Parallel MCP, You.com, DuckDuckGo, Kimi, Anysearch, xAI, Mistral, Bright Data, SerpBase, SerpApi, Serper, Serply, Valyu, XCrawl, Baizhi) remain excluded; omit this field to use the configured provider, or use auto when none is configured"},"workflow":{"type":"string","enum":["none","summary-review","auto-summary"],"description":"Search workflow mode: none = no curator (default), summary-review = open curator with auto summary draft, auto-summary = generate summary without opening curator"},"proxy":{"type":"string","description":"http(s) or socks proxy URL (e.g. http://host:port or socks5h://host:port) used for every outbound request in this call (search APIs and content fetches). Node fetch ignores HTTP(S)_PROXY env vars, so set this (or `proxy` in web-search.json) when direct access is blocked; empty string forces direct access."}}}},{"name":"source_check","description":"Gather web sources for a claim and return a bounded machine-readable research artifact with exact passage citations for manual review.","parameters":{"type":"object","required":["claim"],"properties":{"claim":{"type":"string","description":"The assertion to gather web sources for."},"queries":{"type":"array","items":{"type":"string"},"description":"Search queries (default: the claim)."},"numResults":{"type":"integer","minimum":1,"maximum":20,"description":"Results per query (default: 5, max: 20)."},"fetchContent":{"type":"boolean","description":"Fetch up to 5 result pages for exact passage extraction."},"recencyFilter":{"type":"string","enum":["day","week","month","year"],"description":"Filter by recency."},"domainFilter":{"type":"array","items":{"type":"string"},"description":"Limit to domains; prefix with - to exclude."},"provider":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string","enum":["auto","all","openai","brave","parallel","parallel-mcp","tinyfish","search1api","searchinfinity","querit","tavily","you","firecrawl","jina","searxng","duckduckgo","perplexity","gemini","kimi","exa","serpdive","kagi","ollama","anysearch","xai","mistral","brightdata","serpbase","serpapi","serper","serply","valyu","bocha","xcrawl","baizhi"]},{"type":"array","items":{"type":"string","enum":["openai","brave","parallel","parallel-mcp","tinyfish","search1api","searchinfinity","querit","tavily","you","firecrawl","jina","searxng","duckduckgo","perplexity","gemini","kimi","exa","serpdive","kagi","ollama","anysearch","xai","mistral","brightdata","serpbase","serpapi","serper","serply","valyu","bocha","xcrawl","baizhi"]},"minItems":1}],"description":"Search provider or non-empty list of allowed providers to search simultaneously; all searches eligible allowed providers (OpenAI, Brave, Parallel, TinyFish, Search1API, Searchinfinity, Querit, Tavily, Firecrawl, Jina, SearXNG, Perplexity, Gemini, Exa, SERPdive, Kagi, Ollama, Bocha); explicit-only allowed providers (Parallel MCP, You.com, DuckDuckGo, Kimi, Anysearch, xAI, Mistral, Bright Data, SerpBase, SerpApi, Serper, Serply, Valyu, XCrawl, Baizhi) remain excluded"},"proxy":{"type":"string","description":"http(s) or socks proxy URL (e.g. http://host:port or socks5h://host:port) used for every outbound request in this call (search APIs and result-page fetches). Empty string forces direct access."}}}},{"name":"fetch_content","description":"Fetch URL(s). Available modes: readable (default): extract readable content as markdown; raw: return the exact textual body using direct HTTP only; answer: answer a prompt using only fetched content. Direct image URLs return resized image content when supported by the selected mode. Supports YouTube transcripts, GitHub repositories, PDFs, and local videos when supported by the selected mode. Full original content is stored for retrieval with get_search_content.","parameters":{"type":"object","properties":{"url":{"type":"string","description":"Single URL to fetch"},"urls":{"type":"array","items":{"type":"string"},"description":"Multiple URLs (parallel)"},"forceClone":{"type":"boolean","description":"Force cloning large GitHub repositories that exceed the size threshold"},"prompt":{"type":"string","description":"Question or instruction for video analysis, or the page-local question required by answer mode."},"mode":{"type":"string","enum":["readable","raw","answer"],"description":"Fetch mode. readable (default): extract readable content as markdown; raw: return the exact textual body using direct HTTP only; answer: answer a prompt using only fetched content."},"answerModel":{"type":"string","description":"Optional provider/model-id override for answer mode. Defaults to fetch.answerProvider + fetch.answerModel when configured, otherwise the current Pi model."},"timestamp":{"type":"string","description":"Extract video frame(s) at a timestamp or time range. Single: '1:23:45', '23:45', or '85' (seconds). Range: '23:41-25:00' extracts evenly-spaced frames across that span (default 6). Use frames with ranges to control density; single+frames uses a fixed 5s interval. YouTube requires yt-dlp + ffmpeg; local videos require ffmpeg. Use a range when you know the approximate area but not the exact moment — you'll get a contact sheet to visually identify the right frame."},"frames":{"type":"integer","minimum":1,"maximum":12,"description":"Number of frames to extract. Use with timestamp range for custom density, with single timestamp to get N frames at 5s intervals, or alone to sample across the entire video. Requires yt-dlp + ffmpeg for YouTube, ffmpeg for local video."},"model":{"type":"string","description":"Override the Gemini model for video/YouTube analysis (e.g. 'gemini-3.6-flash'). Defaults to config or gemini-3.6-flash."},"auth":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"boolean"}],"description":"Opt into an authFetch profile for local browser-cookie fetching. Use a profile name, or true only when exactly one profile exists."},"proxy":{"type":"string","description":"http(s) or socks proxy URL (e.g. http://host:port or socks5h://host:port) used for this fetch. Needed when the target is unreachable directly; localhost and NO_PROXY hosts always bypass the proxy. Empty string forces direct access."}}}},{"name":"get_search_content","description":"Retrieve bounded pages of full stored search results or fetched content, or find matching passages, from a previous web_search, source_check, or fetch_content call.","parameters":{"type":"object","required":["responseId"],"properties":{"responseId":{"type":"string","description":"The responseId from web_search, source_check, or fetch_content"},"query":{"type":"string","description":"Get content for this query (web_search)"},"queryIndex":{"type":"integer","minimum":0,"description":"Get content for query at index"},"url":{"type":"string","description":"Get content for this URL"},"urlIndex":{"type":"integer","minimum":0,"description":"Get content for URL at index"},"offset":{"type":"integer","minimum":0,"description":"Character offset in stored search or fetched URL content (default 0). Ignored when findText is supplied."},"limit":{"type":"integer","minimum":1,"maximum":30000,"description":"Requested maximum stored-content characters (default and max use maxInlineContentChars). Search-page continuation guidance shares the global output cap and may reduce returnedChars. Ignored when findText is supplied."},"findText":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string","minLength":1,"maxLength":500},{"type":"array","items":{"type":"string","minLength":1,"maxLength":500},"minItems":1,"maxItems":10}],"description":"Text or texts to find in the selected stored content. When supplied, offset and limit are ignored."},"findMode":{"type":"string","enum":["exact","case-insensitive","fuzzy"],"description":"Matching mode for findText (default: case-insensitive). Requires findText."}}}},{"name":"subagent","description":"Delegate one child with {agent,task?}; otherwise set exactly one workflow source, with optional args.\nWorkflow script: write it as one ```js workflow block in this reply, then call subagent({workflow:true,...}). workflow:'./path.js' (any value with '/') loads a file from request cwd; other strings name a resource.\nagent/task exclude workflow; task excludes action. agent may target management actions. action is management/control; validate accepts workflow:true or a path without launching.\nScripts: JavaScript statement bodies with explicit return, top-level await, plain helpers/Promise chains; nested async function/arrow/method helpers are rejected. Await runs.run('key',{agent,task}) before .output; await runs.all([{key,agent,task},...]) for an ordered array, not a key map. Observe every stored run promise with direct await, Promise.race or Promise.all. Await/return runs.steer(key,message,options?) for a prior key, never raw run ids; queued/delivered/missed/failed receipts are not compliance proof.\nBefore advanced orchestration (runs.lanes, rolling fanout, mission state, handoffs), read {action:\"guide\",topic:\"workflows\"} or the pi-subagents skill. Raw-script sandboxes add deeply frozen args; all sandboxes provide runs, emit, console, JavaScript and enabled mission state, with no filesystem/shell/Pi tools/host globals. External CLI agents support native options only when their runner declares them; read guide tool-reference before passing model, structured output, acceptance/agentContract, tool budget, fast, fork context or skills/tools.\nModel override: first call {action:\"models\"}; copy exact provider/id, not agent names. Thinking uses model suffix, not watchdog-only thinking.\nNamed resources: {workflow:'review',args:{task:'...'}} or {workflow:'run-ci',args:{command:'npm test'}}. Raw scripts also accept bounded plain-data args; raw-script args persist as evidence, so never include secrets. worktree:true requires clean source; baseRef defaults to HEAD at allocation or a supported named ref, never full 40/64-character commit IDs or revision expressions.\n\nSAFETY-CRITICAL SUBAGENT GUIDANCE:\n• Direct parent execution is the default. Invoke subagents only when delegation is authorized by the operator's current request or applicable user/project instructions; task size, complexity, risk, tool-call count, or recipe fit do not independently authorize delegation.\n• First call {action:\"list\",capabilities:true}: executable, non-disabled agents only; external-cli requires runner.available === true. Passive PATH/PATHEXT/X_OK is not authentication/version/launch proof; preflight is authoritative.\n• Workflow, child launch, prompt runtime, extension load or child tooling failure is a lane infrastructure blocker. Stop; report exact failure, run/status and repo/cwd/worktree/branch/ref; verify clean worktree or capture partial diff before same-protocol retry or asking the owner. Never silently switch to interactive_shell, pi -ne, Codex/Claude/Cursor CLI or foreground/external mode: governed-workflow fallback requires explicit owner approval, not Pi core's generic pi -ne hint. Explicit foreground/CLI requests and work outside that protocol remain valid.\n• Omit action for execution. For an authorized delegated multi-step/parallel workflow: exactly one top-level subagent workflow call with async:true; children launch only inside it.\n• Async follows asyncByDefault (normally true); async:false only to block the parent, not for final reviews/gates. Consume results at dependency barriers. Native async completion wakes this session: return control, no sleep/poll or bg_wait merely for a wake. bg_wait is for provider/detached work without native notification needing a same-turn result.\n• Ordinary child subagents are not orchestrators; only configured fanout within depth/session limits. For an authorized delegated workflow, keep one writer per cwd/worktree and isolate concurrent writers. Use fresh-context read-only reviewers when independent review was requested, then parent synthesis/fixes. Oracle/advisor unknowns use supervisor dialogue; one-shot only when requested.\n• Bind durable output on runs.run/runs.all, not task filename prose; return actual outputReference/outputPathMapping/artifactPaths, evidence and residual risks.\n• children.list is workflow-only, not an exhaustive list of direct native children: resume only resumable rows. When an intended child's exact run id is known, inspect it with {action:\"status\",id}; if status identifies the candidate, attempt {action:\"resume\",id,message}. Resume authoritatively checks eligibility, may reject it, and otherwise detaches a follow-up/challenge with the stored agent/model/tool contract. Use a labeled same-role fallback only when no known candidate exists or resume rejects eligibility. Scripts await runs.run(newKey,{resume:runId,task}); continue from latest returned runId. Each distinct resume pass needs a new stable key; same-key reuse requires identical launch parameters.\n• Named resources own authority; raw scripts (workflow:true or a path) cannot use runs.host. Granted commands/relative outputs use workflow cwd, never per-step cwd.\n• Inspect asyncId/asyncDir (status.json, events.jsonl, logs) with status/debug.run; control with interrupt/stop/resume/steer. Read {action:\"guide\",topic:\"tool-reference\"} for controls/evidence gates.","parameters":{"type":"object","properties":{"agent":{"type":"string","description":"One-child agent or management target."},"task":{"type":"string","description":"One-child task; requires agent."},"extensionBindings":{"type":"object","maxProperties":16,"additionalProperties":true,"description":"Child-only plain JSON; package.name/1; depth 16, 256 props, 16 KiB."},"action":{"type":"string","minLength":1,"description":"Management/control only; omit for execution. validate accepts workflow: true or a script path. Discover actions with guide topic tool-reference."},"capabilities":{"type":"boolean","description":"list: compact capability rows/details without system prompts."},"name":{"type":"string","description":"schedule.create name."},"id":{"type":"string","description":"Run id/prefix for status/control."},"runId":{"type":"string","description":"Target run ID; prefer id."},"dir":{"type":"string","description":"Async directory for status/control."},"handoffPath":{"type":"string","description":"Existing manifest for worktree/lane actions."},"repo":{"type":"string","description":"worktree.cleanup repo; default cwd."},"planId":{"type":"string","description":"Reserved; cleanup is plan-only."},"laneId":{"type":"string","minLength":1,"maxLength":128,"description":"Exact manifest run id for lane actions."},"merge":{"type":"object","additionalProperties":true,"description":"lane.recordMerge evidence; read guide tool-reference."},"supersession":{"type":"object","additionalProperties":true,"description":"lane.recordSupersession evidence; read guide tool-reference."},"index":{"type":"integer","minimum":0,"description":"Zero-based child/transcript index."},"childId":{"type":"string","minLength":1,"maxLength":256,"description":"Child-scoped stop identity."},"view":{"type":"string","enum":["fleet","transcript"],"description":"status view: fleet overview or transcript tail with id/dir and optional index."},"lines":{"type":"integer","minimum":1,"maximum":500,"description":"Transcript tail lines; default 80."},"topic":{"type":"string"},"message":{"type":"string","description":"resume/steer guidance or project.open prompt."},"mode":{"type":"string","enum":["steer","follow_up","auto","plan","apply"],"description":"steer delivery mode; worktree.cleanup supports plan only, no apply/removal."},"steeringRecovery":{"type":"boolean","description":"steer: pause/revive after missed acknowledgment; default true in direct steer mode, forced false by extension RPC for exact ownership."},"additional":{"type":"integer","minimum":1,"description":"grant-spawn-budget: root interactive parent + native user confirmation only; total grants capped at original configured cap."},"scope":{"type":"string","enum":["session","user","project"],"description":"watchdog.configure scope; default session, persistent only if explicit."},"target":{"type":"string","enum":["main","children","child"],"description":"Watchdog target."},"focus":{"type":"boolean","description":"Focus inspector.open/project.open pane."},"thinking":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"boolean"}],"description":"watchdog.configure only: off/minimal/low/medium/high/xhigh/max, inherit, false=off; true invalid. Dispatch ignores this; use model suffix."},"at":{"type":"string","description":"schedule.create: delay (+10m) or zoned ISO timestamp."},"every":{"type":"string","description":"schedule.create interval, e.g. 30m/6h/2d/2w."},"sessionOnly":{"type":"boolean"},"quiet":{"type":"boolean"},"on":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"integer"}],"description":"Reserved calendar selector."},"timezone":{"type":"string"},"overlap":{"type":"string","enum":["skip"]},"catchUp":{"type":"string","enum":["none","latest"],"description":"Missed schedule occurrences; default latest."},"missionId":{"type":"string","description":"Existing mission to attach to a workflow or schedule.create; read guide missions for lifecycle and retention."},"mission":{"anyOf":[{"type":"object","additionalProperties":true},{"type":"boolean"}],"description":"false disables; true invalid. Object: exactly one non-empty title or summary; objective/labels optional; goal only true, requires budget.tokens."},"missionUpdate":{"type":"object","additionalProperties":true,"description":"Mission patch; read guide missions."},"missionStatus":{"type":"string"},"missionScope":{"type":"string","description":"project (default) or global pointer index."},"runMode":{"type":"string","description":"Attached run mode."},"runStatus":{"type":"string","description":"Attached run status."},"summary":{"type":"string","description":"Mission close summary."},"config":{"anyOf":[{"type":"object","additionalProperties":true},{"type":"string"}],"description":"create/update agent config; object or JSON string."},"workflow":{"anyOf":[{"type":"boolean"},{"type":"string","minLength":1}],"description":"true: run the one ```js workflow block written in this same reply (false invalid). String with '/': script file read from request cwd. Other string: named workflow resource. Raw scripts (true or path) have no runs.host."},"args":{"type":"object","maxProperties":16,"additionalProperties":true,"description":"Plain-JSON args for workflow; 16 fields/object, 64 items/array, depth 8, 16 KiB total; raw-script args are exposed deeply frozen and persisted, so do not include secrets."},"globalConcurrencyLimit":{"type":"integer","minimum":1},"maxSubagentSpawnsPerRun":{"type":"integer","minimum":1},"preflight":{"type":"object","required":["version","lanes"],"properties":{"version":{"type":"integer","minimum":1,"maximum":1},"coverage":{"type":"string","enum":["complete","partial"]},"lanes":{"type":"array","items":{"type":"object","required":["key"],"properties":{"key":{"type":"string","minLength":1,"maxLength":128},"mode":{"type":"string","enum":["mutation","review","scout","gate"]},"decision":{"type":"string","maxLength":256},"claims":{"type":"array","items":{"type":"string","maxLength":256},"maxItems":16},"expectedOutput":{"type":"string","maxLength":256},"independence":{"type":"string","maxLength":256}},"additionalProperties":false},"maxItems":64}},"additionalProperties":false,"description":"workflow: true or a script path only; display-only hints; coverage warns."},"chatProgress":{"type":"string","enum":["auto","off","live-card"],"description":"auto: live card only for watched foreground in same Git repository. live-card requires same-repo async:false; async: omit or auto/off."},"isolation":{"type":"string","enum":["none","worktree"],"description":"Shared cwd or managed git worktrees."},"worktree":{"type":"boolean","description":"Isolate each workflow child in a managed git worktree; child worktree:false overrides default."},"baseRef":{"type":"string"},"lane":{"type":"object","required":["version","key"],"properties":{"version":{"type":"integer","minimum":1,"maximum":1},"key":{"type":"string","minLength":1,"maxLength":128,"pattern":"^[A-Za-z0-9][A-Za-z0-9._-]{0,127}$"},"mode":{"type":"string","enum":["mutation","review","scout","gate"]},"sourceRef":{"type":"string","minLength":1,"maxLength":128},"claims":{"type":"array","items":{"type":"string","minLength":1,"maxLength":160},"maxItems":20},"outputPaths":{"type":"array","items":{"type":"string","minLength":1,"maxLength":256},"maxItems":10}},"additionalProperties":false,"description":"Display/triage only; sourceRef is opaque, never resolved by status."},"context":{"type":"string","enum":["fresh","fork","profile"],"description":"fresh/fork overrides every child; profile requires agent's declared defaultContext, ignoring config. Omitted: defaultSubagentContext wins over each agent defaultContext; implicit fork needs persisted parent + leaf, else fresh. forkContext may prune forks before spawn."},"async":{"type":"boolean","description":"Background; default asyncByDefault. false only to block parent."},"timeoutMs":{"type":"integer","minimum":1,"description":"Foreground and single async runs use config timeoutMs, else 30m; async composites have no default parent deadline. Alias maxRuntimeMs; must agree."},"maxRuntimeMs":{"type":"integer","minimum":1,"description":"Alias timeoutMs (same defaults)."},"checkpointBeforeDeadlineMs":{"type":"integer","minimum":1,"maximum":2147483647,"description":"Async single-agent runs only: the runner requests that the child checkpoint and stop this many ms before the run deadline (best-effort; the deadline kill still applies)."},"toolTimeoutMs":{"type":"integer","minimum":1,"description":"Per-tool deadline (ms); fast builtins default 5m."},"toolBudget":{"type":"object","required":["hard"],"properties":{"soft":{"type":"integer","minimum":1},"hard":{"type":"integer","minimum":1},"block":{"anyOf":[{"type":"array","minItems":1,"items":{"type":"string","minLength":1}},{"type":"string","enum":["*"]}]}},"additionalProperties":false,"description":"soft <= hard; after hard block read/grep/find/ls or '*' for all."},"usageBudget":{"type":"object","properties":{"tokens":{"type":"object","required":["hard"],"properties":{"soft":{"type":"number","exclusiveMinimum":0},"hard":{"type":"number","exclusiveMinimum":0}},"additionalProperties":false},"costUsd":{"type":"object","required":["hard"],"properties":{"soft":{"type":"number","exclusiveMinimum":0},"hard":{"type":"number","exclusiveMinimum":0}},"additionalProperties":false}},"additionalProperties":false,"minProperties":1,"description":"tokens or costUsd; soft <= hard. Root-only reported usage; blocks launches. Running children are not stopped."},"agentScope":{"type":"string","description":"user/project/both (default); project wins collisions."},"cwd":{"type":"string","description":"Execution/project-pane directory."},"machine":{"type":"string","minLength":1,"maxLength":128,"description":"Herdr saved machine id or label; runs an external CLI agent there. cwd then means the directory on that machine."},"artifacts":{"type":"boolean","description":"Debug artifacts; default true."},"includeProgress":{"type":"boolean","description":"Full result progress; default false."},"share":{"type":"boolean","description":"Upload session to GitHub Gist; default false."},"sessionDir":{"type":"string","description":"Session log directory; default temp, independent of share."},"control":{"type":"object","properties":{"enabled":{"type":"boolean"},"needsAttentionAfterMs":{"type":"integer","minimum":1},"activeNoticeAfterMs":{"type":"integer","minimum":1},"activeNoticeAfterTurns":{"type":"integer","minimum":1},"activeNoticeAfterTokens":{"type":"integer","minimum":1},"failedToolAttemptsBeforeAttention":{"type":"integer","minimum":1},"notifyOn":{"type":"array","items":{"type":"string","enum":["active_long_running","needs_attention"]}},"notifyChannels":{"type":"array","items":{"type":"string","enum":["event","async","intercom"]}}}},"output":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"boolean"}],"description":"Child output path or false; relative workflow paths use managed artifact routing. Bind durable output here, not task prose; return outputReference/outputPathMapping/artifactPaths."},"outputMode":{"type":"string","enum":["inline","file-only"],"description":"Default inline; file-only requires output path."},"skill":{"anyOf":[{"type":"array","items":{"type":"string"}},{"type":"boolean"},{"type":"string"}],"description":"Skills: names/CSV/array; false disables, true uses default."},"model":{"type":"string","description":"Child model provider/id; bare id only if unique. Suffix :off/minimal/low/medium/high/xhigh/max overrides agent thinking default."},"fast":{"type":"boolean","description":"Native OpenAI-Codex priority tier; default false, may cost more/quota."},"outputSchema":{"anyOf":[{"type":"object","additionalProperties":true},{"type":"boolean"}],"description":"Structured output schema override; false disables an agent default."},"agentContract":{"type":"object","required":["version"],"properties":{"version":{"type":"integer","minimum":1,"maximum":1}},"additionalProperties":false,"description":"Compatibility behavior. Omit for the default behavior."},"acceptance":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string","enum":["auto","attested","checked"]},{"type":"string","enum":["reviewed"],"deprecated":true},{"type":"string","pattern":"^\\s*\\{[\\s\\S]*$"},{"type":"boolean"},{"type":"object","additionalProperties":true}],"description":"Evidence policy; omit for read-only/review. false disables; true invalid. Prefer object; see guide tool-reference for levels, evidence and review.required."},"gate":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string","minLength":1},{"type":"object","properties":{"command":{"type":"string","minLength":1},"output":{"type":"string","enum":["json"]},"schema":{"type":"object"},"timeoutMs":{"type":"integer","minimum":1}},"required":["command"],"additionalProperties":false}],"description":"Host gate command run after the child finishes: a string, or { command, output: \"json\", schema?, timeoutMs? } whose passing stdout becomes structuredOutput (not with outputSchema). Cannot be combined with acceptance; an explicit acceptance of false is treated as omitted."}}}}],"timestamp":1792212140646}}
