{"type":"message","id":"b75978db","parentId":"c38d02a7","timestamp":"2026-10-17T04:42:23.481Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_eb6af9a7236f48ba879dfe144e298aa7|fc_0b16ddefcb78f265016ad2fcaeab648191b95fd367ebe3f215","name":"read","arguments":{"path":"/Users/alex/.pi/agent/sessions/--Users-alex-.herdr-worktrees-entire-brain-alex-manage-dependencies--/subagent-artifacts/outputs/503ac0e1-a263-4f2c-9698-4d29e3d93021/cor2164/assessment.md","offset":null,"limit":null}}],"api":"openai-codex-responses","provider":"openai-codex","model":"gpt-6.1-sol","usage":{"input":38861,"output":94,"cacheRead":18688,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":0,"totalTokens":57643,"cost":{"input":0.077722,"output":0.0009400000000000001,"cacheRead":0.0018688,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0.0805308}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1792212140945,"responseId":"resp_0b16ddefcb78f265016ad2fcad75e481918ec215137dcd88cb","rawStopReason":"completed","durationMs":2535,"thinkingLevel":"medium"}}
{"type":"message","id":"af6a760e","parentId":"b75978db","timestamp":"2026-10-17T04:42:23.521Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_eb6af9a7236f48ba879dfe144e298aa7|fc_0b16ddefcb78f265016ad2fcaeab648191b95fd367ebe3f215","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"# COR-2164 assessment (read-only)\n\n## Executive summary\nAt immutable main `c8624c8826e4d2632510b46041a54694a0504650`, `scripts/bench` resolves `node-llama-cpp@3.22.1` → `simple-git@3.36.0` → scoped `@simple-git/argv-parser@1.1.1` (not the unscoped package). This is vulnerable code present for GHSA-v5rq-49vh-5v5c; occurrence/reachability are distinct. The visible embed server uses `getLlama()`, loads a configured GGUF, and performs embeddings. Its request data flows to `getEmbeddingFor`, not an evident Git API. This is evidence against ordinary inference triggering the vulnerable parser, not proof the dependency is unreachable: node-llama-cpp install/build/fallback operations remain an unclosed edge, and no exact upstream source/package archive inspection was completed here. Exploitability in this benchmark threat model is therefore **not demonstrated**, rather than ruled out. Parent should treat as a narrowly scoped benchmark tooling exposure pending verification of the package's concrete Git caller path and trust boundary.\n\n## Version/ref matrix\n| Artifact | Exact immutable-main evidence | Result |\n|---|---|---|\n| `scripts/bench/package.json` | `c8624c8:scripts/bench/package.json:6-8` | direct `node-llama-cpp: ^3` |\n| lock node-llama-cpp | `...:scripts/bench/package-lock.json:1155-1160` | 3.22.1; registry tarball; `hasInstallScript: true` |\n| lock its simple-git parent | `...:package-lock.json:1175-1185` | node-llama-cpp dependency `simple-git: ^3.36.0` |\n| lock simple-git | `...:package-lock.json:1444-1454` | 3.36.0; parser range `^1.1.0` |\n| lock parser | `...:package-lock.json:451-458` | scoped `@simple-git/argv-parser` 1.1.1 |\n\nThe scoped parser is the advisory component. Do not infer it is the separate unscoped `argv-parser` package. Dependency change was inherited, not introduced by the dependency-update group (per supplied task context).\n\n## Advisory status and patch\nGHSA-v5rq-49vh-5v5c: supplied prior verification reports CRITICAL, non-withdrawn, affected `<2.0.1`, fixed `2.0.1`; root cause is unsafe-editor detection omitting `VISUAL`. I could not independently re-query live GHSA/OSV/registry endpoints during this pass; thus these ranges/status and the exact patch behavior are reported from supplied verified context, not newly confirmed live evidence. Other flagged simple-git leads needing independent triage: GHSA-858h-whjf-mvg5, GHSA-g4wm-2vf7-vfgr, GHSA-x6jw-m9v5-85vh. This assessment does not validate their ranges/conditions or resolve them as applicable.\n\nRequired patch evidence for a final determination: compare published scoped parser 1.1.1 source to fixed 2.0.1, identify precisely which Git operations invoke editor handling, inspect upstream simple-git 3.x supported range and node-llama-cpp current compatible release/lock/provenance. Do not apply simple-git 4 override without compatibility evidence.\n\n## Call/dataflow and trust boundary\nImmutable source: [`scripts/bench/embed-server.mjs`](https://github.com/entireio/entire-brain/blob/c8624c8826e4d2632510b46041a54694a0504650/scripts/bench/embed-server.mjs) imports `getLlama` (line 19), calls it (line 42), loads `process.env.GGUF` (line 43), creates embedding context (44), then accepts HTTP requests (lines 57 onward) and passes only parsed `input` text to `ctx.getEmbeddingFor(input)` (lines 85-95). Host defaults to loopback (lines 23-25), but operator can set `HOST=0.0.0.0`; no auth on embedding route is apparent. Model path is operator-configured. User-controlled network embedding text is therefore an attacker-controlled input to inference, but no observed source path takes that text to Git.\n\nManifest/lock prove node-llama-cpp is installed transitively with an install script and simple-git is in its dependency graph; they do not prove which install-time or runtime operations invoke simple-git, what arguments/configs are used, nor whether source-build fallback runs Git. `getLlama()` is a dynamic library boundary; graph coverage of repository code cannot resolve transitive npm internals. The decisive unproven edge is **node-llama-cpp@3.22.1 install/prebuilt/source-fallback code → simple-git method → parser's unsafe editor operation**. The server's normal embedding input path does not establish that edge; neither does absence of an application-level simple-git import.\n\nFor this advisory, the trust question is whether untrusted input or less-trusted CI/job config can control `VISUAL` (and operation inputs) in a process with credentials/filesystem authority, causing simple-git to run the unsafe editor command. A developer setting their own `VISUAL` is not itself proof of a privilege boundary; conversely, an attacker-controlled CI environment with repository credentials would materially alter risk. No such boundary/exploit was demonstrated here. Safe-editor guard, opt-in controls, exact arguments and fixed release implementation remain unverified in this pass.\n\n## Compatible remediation/validation options\n1. Prefer a published node-llama-cpp 3.x release whose resolved lock demonstrably brings simple-git 3.x with scoped parser ≥2.0.1. Validate from official release source/metadata and exact lock; inspect install scripts and prebuilt/fallback code, then compatibility on supported platforms (without relying on semver alone).\n2. If upstream parent has not updated, request/confirm upstream 3.x patch or use a narrowly scoped parser override only after proving simple-git 3.x compatibility with parser 2.x APIs and exercising editor-related call contracts in a controlled CI validation. Do not force simple-git 4.\n3. If Git is only a source-build fallback, operationally require verified prebuilt platform artifacts and disable/avoid fallback where supported; validate platform matrix and failure behavior. This reduces exposure, not a substitute for fixed dependency.\n\nValidation should cover lock graph/provenance, prebuilt and source-build behavior by platform, exact simple-git call sites/arguments, and editor safety for `VISUAL` absent/present with safe and unsafe operations. No installs, builds, tests, or vulnerable code execution were performed.\n\n## Confidence, limitations, tool coverage\nHigh confidence in immutable lock versions and visible embed-server flow; medium confidence that ordinary inference input is not directly routed to Git; low confidence on install/build reachability and exploitability because exact npm tarball/source and live advisory/release evidence were not inspected. No conclusion that vulnerability is unreachable or safe.\n\nBrain capability preflight/brief/recall attempted; installed `entire` CLI has no `brain` command, so Brain unavailable. Supplied context says parent KB searches found no relevant notes. Graph v0.4.1-nightly provider available with JavaScript call relations; query was run, but this checkout is the parent's old branch (`2a05be9`), not requested immutable main. Per instruction no graph result from that old HEAD is used as evidence about current main; the supplied fixed paths and immutable `git show` source/lock were used instead. No live network/API queries or dynamic execution were performed."}],"isError":false,"durationMs":40,"timestamp":1792212143521}}
