{"type":"message","id":"5968434e","parentId":"284c45fb","timestamp":"2026-10-17T04:44:04.969Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_cfdcf593b2d44c22b552b73f0e0905d4|fc_0b16ddefcb78f265016ad2fd12cb148191acce5b0e5ab0af4c","name":"read","arguments":{"path":"/Users/alex/.pi/agent/sessions/--Users-alex-.herdr-worktrees-entire-brain-alex-manage-dependencies--/subagent-artifacts/outputs/a49a6ba0-bb75-46cb-84d9-ce78347a2ec1/cor2145/trace-assessment.md","offset":null,"limit":null}},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_1c6011a8bcb049ca8ff24222092b9778|fc_0b16ddefcb78f265016ad2fd14bb8881918b3ae2a0add96bab","name":"read","arguments":{"path":"/Users/alex/.pi/agent/sessions/--Users-alex-.herdr-worktrees-entire-brain-alex-manage-dependencies--/subagent-artifacts/outputs/a49a6ba0-bb75-46cb-84d9-ce78347a2ec1/cor2164/trace-assessment.md","offset":null,"limit":null}}],"api":"openai-codex-responses","provider":"openai-codex","model":"gpt-6.1-sol","usage":{"input":1709,"output":200,"cacheRead":64000,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":0,"totalTokens":65909,"cost":{"input":0.003418,"output":0.002,"cacheRead":0.0064,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0.011818}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1792212241004,"responseId":"resp_0b16ddefcb78f265016ad2fd1183f48191befb25c86ed27342","rawStopReason":"completed","durationMs":3965,"thinkingLevel":"medium"}}
{"type":"message","id":"a7aff653","parentId":"5968434e","timestamp":"2026-10-17T04:44:04.971Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_cfdcf593b2d44c22b552b73f0e0905d4|fc_0b16ddefcb78f265016ad2fd12cb148191acce5b0e5ab0af4c","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"# COR-2145 — setup-go bundled XML parser trace\n\n## Executive assessment\n\n**Vulnerable code is present** in the exact action currently referenced by main: `actions/setup-go@v7` resolves to commit `b7ad1dad31e06c5925ef5d2fc7ad053ef454303e`; its lock pins `fast-xml-parser@5.9.3` and `@azure/core-xml@1.5.1`. Current GitHub advisory API confirms GHSA-8r6m-32jq-jx6q is active HIGH, `>=5.9.3 <5.10.1`, fixed `5.10.1`; summary: repeated DOCTYPE declarations reset entity expansion limits. **Reachability through an Azure XML parse exists in the included Azure blob SDK family, but attacker-controlled repeated-DOCTYPE input to setup-go's executed service operations is not demonstrated. Exploitability in these workflows is therefore not demonstrated.** The archive/model/repository-input path is binary or JSON, not XML by design/source evidence below; the remaining trust question is whether a lower-trust actor can control the XML body from the service/proxy/cache response on these jobs.\n\nSmallest currently available option: replace `@v7` with the exact immutable ref for an official setup-go commit whose lock and distributed entrypoint demonstrably carry >=5.10.1, if such a release exists. At the point inspected, latest official action release was `v7.0.0` (July 16 2026) and tag `v7` resolves to the same vulnerable commit; no fixed setup-go release was found. Thus **no verified upstream action-level fix was identified**. Do not assume pinning package version alone fixes the checked-in bundled action.\n\n## Exact refs and package state\n\n- Current tree `c8624c8826e4d2632510b46041a54694a0504650`: `.github/workflows/test.yml` uses setup-go `@v7` with Go `1.27.2`; `.github/workflows/nightly.yml` uses `@v7` with `1.27.x`. (Immutable tree inspection.)\n- GitHub `GET /repos/actions/setup-go/git/ref/tags/v7`: tag object is commit `b7ad1dad31e06c5925ef5d2fc7ad053ef454303e`.\n- Official source archive inspected as static data from `https://api.github.com/repos/actions/setup-go/tarball/b7ad1dad31e06c5925ef5d2fc7ad053ef454303e`; action metadata declares `dist/setup/index.js` (also v7 bundle API blob SHA `187253e6f2118f85ec516374beed40d983a70015`).\n- v7 `package-lock.json`: `fast-xml-parser` 5.9.3; `@azure/core-xml` 1.5.1; `@actions/cache` 6.2.0; `@azure/storage-blob` 12.32.0. Azure storage blob depends on core-xml (`^1.4.5`); core-xml depends on parser (`^5.5.9`).\n- Prior baseline exact v6.4.0: lock has fast-xml-parser 5.4.1, below advisory range. This establishes that the upgrade moved into the affected range.\n\nLive API evidence: `https://api.github.com/advisories/GHSA-8r6m-32jq-jx6q` returned summary, severity, null `withdrawn_at`, affected range and first patched version. npm metadata for `fast-xml-parser@5.10.1` identifies registry tarball and upstream gitHead `2ce7191ef0f96473b8c7e0c384fbc4ad34a222de`. Package metadata is not proof setup-go released a bundle containing it. GitHub releases endpoint's five latest entries showed v7.0.0 then v6.5.0; no fixed newer setup-go release was evidenced in those results. Upstream package source/release: `https://github.com/NaturalIntelligence/fast-xml-parser/releases/tag/v5.10.1`; exact npm tarball `https://registry.npmjs.org/fast-xml-parser/-/fast-xml-parser-5.10.1.tgz`. The upstream history includes DOCTYPE fix commit `57ad0d3388959cc7bfaed3e1debdee05053f9f1b` (GitHub commit API). The patch's exact line diff was not captured here, so no claim about its implementation details beyond the advisory's stated fix.\n\n## Source dataflow and boundary\n\nStatic upstream v7 source archive was extracted as data, not installed or executed. Source paths below are relative to `actions-setup-go` commit above:\n\n1. `src/main.ts`: `run()` reads the Go version input, `go-download-base-url` or `GO_DOWNLOAD_BASE_URL`, and `cache` input; it invokes setup/install and the optional dependency cache. Workflow does not set a custom download base URL or enable action cache explicitly; action metadata/defaults need to be considered for omitted cache input.\n2. `src/installer.ts`: checks runner tool cache (`tc.find`); on miss downloads metadata and tool. Normal manifest path calls `getManifest(...)`; fallback `getManifestFromURL()` uses toolkit `HttpClient.getJson(MANIFEST_URL)` (JSON). Go dist version listing uses the Go download manifest/listing (`getVersionsDist`) and returns metadata fields such as filename/platform/arch/download URL. Actual Go payload goes through `tc.downloadTool(info.downloadUrl, ...)`, then archive extraction. This input is archive bytes; it is not handed to XML parser in setup-go source. Custom URL option can change metadata/download origin, but current workflows do not pass it; environment override `GO_DOWNLOAD_BASE_URL` remains a possible runner-level influence, not a repository-controlled workflow field shown here.\n3. `src/cache-restore.ts` invokes `@actions/cache.restoreCache` on Go module/build cache paths, and `src/cache-save.ts` invokes `@actions/cache.saveCache`. `src/package-managers.ts` identifies `go.mod`, `go env GOMODCACHE`, and `go env GOCACHE`. Cache archive content is restored to filesystem paths, not directly parsed as XML by setup-go. In fork PRs cache save is generally read-only/refused; that does not itself establish restore provenance or parser input.\n4. The shipped dependency graph includes `@actions/cache -> @azure/storage-blob -> @azure/core-xml -> fast-xml-parser`. Core-xml 1.5.1 `dist/esm/xml.js` `parseXML(str, opts)` validates XML and calls `new XMLParser(getParserOptions(opts)).parse(str)` (lines 54–64 of that npm archive). Parser options include `processEntities: true`, `parseTagValue: false`, `parseAttributeValue: false`, `trimValues: false`; no explicit option disabling DOCTYPE is set. This is a concrete vulnerable parse capability, not proof a specific network response reaches it.\n5. `@actions/cache` 6.2.0 source `lib/internal/downloadUtils.js` calls `client.getProperties()` during cache download; this is Azure Blob SDK surface. Inspection did not find direct `parseXML` calls in cache library source. Azure SDK transport/deserialization is transitive/generated and the static pass did not yet resolve each operation response to the precise `core-xml.parseXML` invocation or determine whether the response body is XML, JSON, or otherwise. Therefore the exact claim “cache service response enters vulnerable parse” remains unproven. Similarly, setup-go's Go metadata is fetched as JSON in the cited source; binary archives are not the XML input. Do not equate cache archives or Go distribution metadata with XML.\n\n**Attacker control:** workflow source establishes PR trigger and low default permission (`contents: read`), plus nightly build job `contents: write`; those facts are not proof of safety or attacker control. A PR author can control repository content and potentially workflow-associated inputs, but here Go version is literal in workflow and cache paths are fixed. Setup action executes before repository build commands. To reach the repeated-DOCTYPE parser, a lower-trust party would need control over a response actually parsed as XML (e.g. an XML service reply, a malicious/compromised endpoint/proxy, or a cache service response). No evidence here establishes that a fork contributor can cause GitHub/Azure's response to contain such XML. Endpoint/proxy environment variables and custom download URL can alter endpoints in some paths; this repository does not set those inputs, but self-hosted runner/environment control and service compromise are outside the evidence. Input-size bounds were not established; vulnerable operation is only relevant if an XML body with repeated DOCTYPE declarations reaches the parser.\n\nBest counterexample to a blanket “unreachable” claim: this action explicitly exercises networked Azure storage-blob code through `@actions/cache`, and the bundled Azure/core-xml/parser chain has a parser configured with entity processing enabled. A server error/response on a blob operation could plausibly traverse XML deserialization; absence of a parser call in action's own source is not a non-reachability proof. Conversely, the attacker-control side of that counterexample is not established.\n\n## Disposition, remediation and confidence\n\n- **Present:** yes, exact pinned v7 commit contains vulnerable parser resolution in runtime lock and bundle dependency tree.\n- **Reachable operation:** XML parser is called in included `@azure/core-xml`; exact setup-go-triggered operation-to-parser caller edge was not fully resolved in this pass. Ordinary setup-go Go-version metadata is JSON, downloaded tool is archive, cache payload is archive. A possible Azure SDK service XML response remains the narrow unresolved edge.\n- **Demonstrated exploitable in workflow:** no. No payload was executed and no attacker-controllable parser input chain established.\n- **Fix:** upstream fast-xml-parser 5.10.1 exists, but current official setup-go v7.0.0 bundle/lock remains at 5.9.3. No verified fixed official action commit/release surfaced in the official latest-release query. Avoid speculative pin to a nonexistent action ref. If an official setup-go release appears with fixed bundled dependency, pin that immutable commit and verify action.yml, lock AND shipped dist. Otherwise seek upstream setup-go maintenance/fix; do not vendor/patch without parent authorization and reachability analysis.\n- Confidence high in tag, lock, source path, versions, and current workflow usage; medium on XML parser settings; limited on exact Azure operation-to-parser edge and attacker control. No install, build, test, exploit, source mutation, or third-party code execution performed. First report remains historical; this report supersedes it only on the additional static investigation above."}],"isError":false,"durationMs":1,"timestamp":1792212244971}}
{"type":"message","id":"bd62f0f0","parentId":"a7aff653","timestamp":"2026-10-17T04:44:04.972Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_1c6011a8bcb049ca8ff24222092b9778|fc_0b16ddefcb78f265016ad2fd14bb8881918b3ae2a0add96bab","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"# COR-2164 — source-level exposure assessment\n\n## Executive summary\nThe transitive component is present and reachable during specific node-llama-cpp source-download/build/clone utilities, but the concrete calls found do **not** invoke the editor operation implicated by GHSA-v5rq-49vh-5v5c. The benchmark's ordinary embedding path uses prebuilt binaries and does not call those repository-management functions. Vulnerability presence is established; exploitability via the benchmark's model input is not demonstrated, and the package's exposed Git paths are predominantly explicit developer/installer operations with operator-selected repository/tag inputs. This substantially narrows exposure versus a generic “transitive dependency” claim.\n\nA compatible upstream route exists without simple-git 4: current node-llama-cpp 3.22.2 still declares simple-git `^3.36.0`, while simple-git current 4.0.2 declares scoped parser 2.0.1. The smallest safe path is to determine whether an official simple-git 3.x release exists that updates parser to >=2.0.1, or obtain upstream confirmation/patch for 3.x; do not force v4 into the node-llama-cpp range. As checked against npm registry, parser dist-tag latest is 2.0.1; node-llama-cpp latest GitHub release was v3.22.2.\n\n## Exact baseline and source evidence\nImmutable project revision `c8624c8826e4d2632510b46041a54694a0504650`:\n- [`scripts/bench/package.json`](https://github.com/entireio/entire-brain/blob/c8624c8826e4d2632510b46041a54694a0504650/scripts/bench/package.json#L6-L8): direct `node-llama-cpp: ^3`.\n- [`scripts/bench/package-lock.json`](https://github.com/entireio/entire-brain/blob/c8624c8826e4d2632510b46041a54694a0504650/scripts/bench/package-lock.json#L1155-L1160): 3.22.1 with install script; lines 1175-1185 record simple-git `^3.36.0` dependency.\n- Same lock lines 1444-1454: simple-git 3.36.0 depends on scoped `@simple-git/argv-parser: ^1.1.0`; lines 451-458 resolve scoped parser 1.1.1. This advisory is about the scoped package, not unscoped `argv-parser`.\n\nDownloaded only as static tarball data into `/tmp/cor2164-static` and inspected without installation/execution: official npm node-llama-cpp 3.22.1, simple-git 3.36.0, scoped parser 1.1.1 and 2.0.1. Package manifest confirms node-llama-cpp 3.22.1 `postinstall: node ./dist/cli/cli.js postinstall`, dependency simple-git `^3.36.0`, and optional platform packages pinned 3.22.1. Registry returned official tarball URLs; integrity should be checked against project lock before treating local archives as matching lock bytes (not completed in this pass).\n\n### Concrete Git operations and trigger\nPublished node-llama-cpp `dist/utils/gitReleaseBundles.js` imports simple-git and uses it to edit the llama.cpp checkout: `fetch --unshallow`, `log -1`, `raw commit-tree`, `reset --hard`, `tags`, `tag --delete`, `branch`, `branch --delete`, `tag`, `raw gc`, and `raw bundle create`; recursive variant additionally `listRemote`, `init`, `addRemote`, `add`, `commit`, `branch -M`, then the same raw Git operations. These are repository-history/bundle operations, not editor invocations.\n\n`dist/bindings/utils/cloneLlamaCppRepo.js` calls simple-git clone for either a local bundle or `https://github.com/${owner}/${repo}.git`, with options `--depth 1`, `--branch tag`, optional `--recursive`, `--quiet`; for bundle clone it removes origin. It also calls `removeRemote`. `ensureLlamaCppRepoIsCloned` selects the default repo/release, resolves release when needed, clones, applies patches. `dist/cli/commands/source/commands/DownloadCommand.js` explicitly exposes `source download` and configurable `--repo`, `--release`, GPU/arch, `--skipBuild`, `--noBundle`; default repo/release can be influenced by `NODE_LLAMA_CPP_REPO` and `NODE_LLAMA_CPP_REPO_RELEASE`. CI-only bundle-metadata flag triggers history rewriting/bundle commands. Config has `enableRecursiveClone=false`.\n\nThe generic cloning entry point uses `--branch` values derived from selected tag; normal Git methods receive no attacker-supplied editor command in these call sites. The history editing commands listed above likewise don't invoke editor (messages are passed as `-m`; branch/tag names are values). The unsafe editor advisory's condition is therefore not reached by the identified operations. `VISUAL` may be hostile in a CI environment, but an environment variable alone cannot make these non-editor operations execute it. A separate simple-git callsite elsewhere in package source invoking `commit()` without a message or another editor operation was not established.\n\n### Normal install/runtime versus build fallback\nInstall script invokes the package CLI postinstall; the static sources show source clone/build facilities and Git helpers. `getLlama()`/model loading is the benchmark runtime path; the project server calls `getLlama`, loads operator-provided `GGUF`, then `getEmbeddingFor(input)`. This user-controlled embedding text does not flow to clone/repository arguments. Package's platform optional dependencies provide prebuilt binaries; source download/build (`source download`, `source build`, and lazy ensure-cloned paths) is a distinct code path. Postinstall settings include `NODE_LLAMA_CPP_POSTINSTALL` (`auto`, `ignoreFailedBuild`, `skip`) and npm config equivalent. The postinstall CLI implementation's exact platform branch was not followed sufficiently to state it never clones: the installed source/build decision is the residual execution edge. However, even when clone/build executes, inspected Git calls do not invoke editor.\n\nTrust boundary: benchmark binds localhost by default and accepts unauthenticated embedding input; operator can expose `HOST=0.0.0.0`. That makes request text attacker-controlled under exposure, but source inspection shows it only reaches inference. Repository URL/ref controls are installer/operator environment or explicit CLI options, not request fields. No evidence establishes a lower-trust attacker setting `NODE_LLAMA_CPP_REPO`, refs, postinstall config, or `VISUAL` in a credentialed privileged process. In CI, repository credentials and environment can raise impact, but examined operations still do not hit editor handling. This is not proof of overall node-llama-cpp safety or of other advisories' non-applicability.\n\n## Advisory checks and fix status\nParent supplied independently verified GHSA-v5rq-49vh-5v5c active CRITICAL, scoped parser `<2.0.1`, fixed 2.0.1 at 2026-10-17T04:39:24Z. Direct unauthenticated GitHub API requests to guessed `steveukx/git-js` advisory endpoints returned HTTP 404 (including all four GHSA ids); this is an endpoint/repository lookup miss, not evidence network/auth failed. The npm archives and registry reads did succeed. Exact security advisory payloads, patch commit, and three other advisory conditions could not be correlated from those 404s; no unsupported range/status claims are made for GHSA-858h-whjf-mvg5, GHSA-g4wm-2vf7-vfgr, GHSA-x6jw-m9v5-85vh. They must be assessed independently against simple-git 3.36.0 code; this run did not establish their applicability.\n\nParser 1.1.1 and 2.0.1 dist bundles/source maps were obtained, but no completed source-map diff/line-level patch analysis in this pass. Main advisory synopsis supplied: unsafe-editor detection omits `VISUAL`. The concrete simple-git operations above do not appear to invoke editor; thus that particular defect lacks a demonstrated entry path from these node-llama-cpp callers.\n\nCurrent official release observations from registry/GitHub: node-llama-cpp latest GitHub release `v3.22.2`; its npm metadata declares simple-git `^3.36.0`. Current simple-git npm latest is 4.0.2 and declares scoped parser 2.0.1. Because node-llama-cpp 3.x range excludes 4.x, simple-git v4 is not a compatible automatic solution. npm registry parser dist-tag latest is 2.0.1. No evidence found here of a newer simple-git 3.x patch release; exact available 3.x versions need registry version-list verification. Do not force parser 2.x independently until its compatibility against simple-git 3.36 parser API is proven; major version alone is not a compatibility guarantee.\n\n## Narrow remediation options\n1. Ask node-llama-cpp upstream to update its simple-git 3.x dependency once a compatible 3.x simple-git release ships with parser 2.0.1, then consume that official 3.x fix and verify resulting lock/provenance.\n2. If upstream confirms parser 2.0.1 API-compatible with simple-git 3.36, a scoped parser override is a candidate but requires contract/API diff and focused tests of parsing and editor behavior; absent that evidence do not recommend as verified.\n3. Since vulnerable operation is not observed in these package callers, document conditional exposure and avoid risky forced major override. Still update other simple-git advisories if their independently verified conditions apply.\n\nValidation for a dependency fix: resolve exact lock graph and registry integrity; compare parser source maps and fixed editor guard; inspect simple-git 3.x methods invoking editor; check node-llama-cpp prebuilt install and explicit source download/build per supported platform, without running third-party code in this investigation.\n\n## Confidence and limitations\nHigh confidence in package versions, declared dependency ranges, and the inspected Git operations/call arguments because they came from official published archives. Moderate-high confidence that the identified node-llama-cpp operations do not invoke an editor, based on full `gitReleaseBundles.js`, `cloneLlamaCppRepo.js` and DownloadCommand caller inspection. Moderate confidence ordinary benchmark model/request inputs do not reach Git. No exploitability demonstrated. Residuals: exact postinstall platform branching and all package simple-git call sites not exhaustively audited; parser source diff not completed; three advisory conditions and authoritative GHSA patch metadata unresolved after exact sanitized 404s; no archive integrity verification against lock. No install, build, tests, scripts, exploit, or third-party code execution occurred.\n\nBrain CLI unavailable: `entire brain` command is absent from installed CLI (preflight attempted); graph provider v0.4.1-nightly is available but current checkout is parent's old HEAD, not requested immutable main. Graph was not used to imply npm package relationships; manual inspection of official static sources closed the key Git-call edge. Network access was functional for npm registry and GitHub release endpoint; GHSA 404s were specific wrong endpoint/repository, not network unavailability."}],"isError":false,"durationMs":1,"timestamp":1792212244972}}
