{"type":"message","id":"313532a2","parentId":"daae5927","timestamp":"2026-10-19T02:01:25.639Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_82c96d291c59410587558725e01a4561|fc_0b16ddefcb78f265016ad579f0b3a4819195eab139c4cf0abe","name":"read","arguments":{"path":"/Users/alex/.claude/skills/herdr-launch/SKILL.md","offset":null,"limit":null}},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_c10cb465a0b94fbe9501d3a7aeedf9b4|fc_0b16ddefcb78f265016ad579f562bc81919ff389b234500fd0","name":"read","arguments":{"path":"/Users/alex/.claude/skills/routing-workspace-agents/SKILL.md","offset":null,"limit":null}},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_b9b96fb3243c4a0ea57bf420a7b7f346|fc_0b16ddefcb78f265016ad579f562bc8191a95297c1921337fb","name":"read","arguments":{"path":"/Users/alex/.claude/skills/orchestrating-work/SKILL.md","offset":null,"limit":null}},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_88222c85dfad4ec7b2b2eb12c3b3ab17|fc_0b16ddefcb78f265016ad579f562bc8191a8f57aa1d890a8fc","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"printf 'HERDR_ENV=%s workspace=%s pane=%s\\n' \"${HERDR_ENV:-}\" \"${HERDR_WORKSPACE_ID:-}\" \"${HERDR_PANE_ID:-}\"; herdr workspace get wGX; herdr workspace list | jq '[.result.workspaces[]|select((.label // \"\")|contains(\"2164\"))|{workspace_id,label,agent_status}]'; git status --porcelain=v1; gh api repos/entireio/entire-brain/git/ref/heads/main --jq .object.sha; /usr/bin/python3 /Users/alex/.local/state/herdr/orchestrations/dependency-followups-20261015/python314-incident/assert-runtime.py; openusage > /Users/alex/.local/state/herdr/orchestrations/security-remediation-wPB-20261018/quota-reloaded.json","timeout":30}},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_7cd1332ac22c4170b1117f54bcea5ffc|fc_0b16ddefcb78f265016ad579f562bc819189558dbd0a5ab3b7","name":"mcp","arguments":{"tool":"linear_get_issue","args":{"id":"COR-2164","includeRelations":true}}},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_1fdc2a78de0c41b4b4afcad0c233bad3|fc_0b16ddefcb78f265016ad579f562bc81919a87da033bb55c77","name":"mcp","arguments":{"tool":"linear_get_issue","args":{"id":"COR-2154","fields":["id","status","title","description","updatedAt"]}}}],"api":"openai-codex-responses","provider":"openai-codex","model":"gpt-6.1-sol","usage":{"input":218,"output":398,"cacheRead":455680,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":0,"totalTokens":456296,"cost":{"input":0.0008719999999999999,"output":0.0059700000000000005,"cacheRead":0.09113600000000001,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0.09797800000000001}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1792375276547,"responseId":"resp_0b16ddefcb78f265016ad579edb6ac8191adeab04f5fd8c417","rawStopReason":"completed","durationMs":9090,"thinkingLevel":"medium"}}
{"type":"message","id":"f85d624e","parentId":"313532a2","timestamp":"2026-10-19T02:01:27.038Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_82c96d291c59410587558725e01a4561|fc_0b16ddefcb78f265016ad579f0b3a4819195eab139c4cf0abe","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"---\nname: herdr-launch\ndescription: Use for the mechanical launch of a routed Pi or Claude agent in a new Herdr worktree workspace.\ncompatibility: Requires herdr CLI, HERDR_ENV=1, git, jq, realpath, and an existing primary repository workspace in Herdr.\n---\n\n# Launch an Agent in a Herdr Worktree\n\nThis skill is mechanical: it does not decompose work, choose a model, or make policy. `orchestrating-work` supplies a selected `workspace-agent-route.v1` receipt and a complete outcome-oriented starter packet. The numbered contract below remains the default implementation-owner launch; private reviews use only the explicit exception below.\n\n## Explicit private review exception\n\nFor a private review of somebody else's Trail, invoke `python3 ~/.claude/skills/herdr-launch/scripts/review_trail.py <canonical-trail-url>` (optionally `--deep`; `--source-workspace-id ID` selects one verified primary workspace when multiple match). This directly runnable helper also backs the tracked Fish `hreview` function; see the Pi configuration README for manual installation. Select a Claude review model with `-m sonnet` or `-m opus` (`--model` also works), independently of `--deep`. Without extra-usage authorization, the helper requires healthy included Claude weekly and session capacity and refuses before mutation if evidence is unavailable or exhausted. Pair the model with `-x` (`--allow-extra-usage`) to explicitly authorize extra usage. The existing `--harness claude --allow-extra-usage` pair remains available and defaults to sonnet, independently of review depth. `--deep` requests broader coverage only; it does not automatically select a higher model. Without explicit `-m opus`, hreview routes normal complexity through the shared router. `--model` and `--harness` cannot be combined; `-x` alone is invalid. The launcher passes every model/harness constraint and `-x` authorization to `route.py` exactly once, then persists and launches the returned receipt unchanged. Explicit extra-usage authorization skips quota collection and records an operator-authorized selection without changing billing settings or persistent override state; it does not bypass provider rate limits. Without a model or override, routing remains quota-aware. It parses the explicit Trail repository, selects the matching primary workspace independently of caller cwd or linked worktree, routes before creation, fetches review-owned refs in the verified source Git store, creates at the Trail **head** SHA, and delivers `/review-others-trail` (Claude) or `/skill:review-others-trail` (Pi), never the ownership skill. Trail base SHA is recorded separately. Failed or ambiguous delivery preserves the workspace and private receipt; ask Alex before retrying a failed launch. No code fixing, publication, approval, or merge authority is conveyed. Model suitability, automation prerequisites and review depth remain solely in `review-others-trail`.\n\nOnly the explicit review command selects this exception. An ordinary implementation launch still uses the numbered ownership-skill contract and its supplied route/packet unchanged.\n\n## Contract\n\nInputs are a selected routing receipt, lowercase hyphenated `slug`, complete `starter_packet`, and optionally a directly verified `source_workspace_id` plus `target_repo_root`. Default the source to the calling workspace. Use branch `alex/<slug>`, label `<slug>`, and a unique `<kind>-<slug>` agent name truncated to 32 valid characters.\n\nThe routing receipt must have `status: selected`, `harness: claude|pi`, and exactly one corresponding model:\n\n- Claude: `sonnet`, `opus`, or `fable`\n- Pi: `openai-codex/gpt-6-luna`, `openai-codex/gpt-6-sol`, or `openai-codex/gpt-6-astra`\n\nSonnet/Luna is the default; Opus/Sol is permitted for complex work without approval.\nBefore launching Fable or Astra, verify Alex explicitly requested/approved that exact\nmodel and the receipt's `requestedModel` matches it. A premium receipt, generic\nrequest for a larger model, or provider override alone is not authorization. If\napproval is missing, ask and stop before creating a workspace or starting an agent.\nDo not substitute Fable for requested Astra or vice versa. Warn about Fable's cost.\n\nTrack:\n\n```text\nsource_validated -> created -> starting -> ready -> prompt_sent -> working\n```\n\n`blocked`, `done`, and `failed` are outcomes. Track `prompt_attempts`; maximum two.\n\n## 1. Validate the source\n\n```bash\ntest \"${HERDR_ENV:-}\" = 1\ntest -n \"${HERDR_WORKSPACE_ID:-}\"\nsource_workspace_id=\"${source_workspace_id:-$HERDR_WORKSPACE_ID}\"\nsource_json=$(herdr workspace get \"$source_workspace_id\")\nprintf '%s\\n' \"$source_json\"\n\nsource_checkout=$(realpath \"$(printf '%s\\n' \"$source_json\" | jq -er \\\n  '.result.workspace.worktree | select(.is_linked_worktree == false) | .checkout_path')\")\nsource_repo_key=$(printf '%s\\n' \"$source_json\" | jq -er \\\n  '.result.workspace.worktree | select(.is_linked_worktree == false) | .repo_key')\nsource_root=$(realpath \"$(git -C \"$source_checkout\" rev-parse --show-toplevel)\")\ntarget_root=$(realpath \"$(git -C \"${target_repo_root:-$PWD}\" rev-parse --show-toplevel)\")\ntest \"$source_root\" = \"$target_root\"\n```\n\nAn explicit source is allowed only when it is an existing primary workspace whose canonical repository root matches the directly verified target root. A linked source fails clearly, whether it is the caller or an explicit target. Never walk to a parent, guess from UI focus, use `--cwd`, run raw `git worktree`, or fall back to `herdr workspace create`.\n\n## 2. Create and verify\n\n```bash\ncreated=$(herdr worktree create \\\n  --workspace \"$source_workspace_id\" \\\n  --branch \"alex/<slug>\" \\\n  --base HEAD \\\n  --label \"<slug>\" \\\n  --no-focus)\nprintf '%s\\n' \"$created\"\n\nworkspace_id=$(printf '%s\\n' \"$created\" | jq -er '.result.workspace.workspace_id')\npane_id=$(printf '%s\\n' \"$created\" | jq -er '.result.root_pane.pane_id')\ncheckout_path=$(realpath \"$(printf '%s\\n' \"$created\" | jq -er '.result.workspace.worktree.checkout_path')\")\ncreated_linked=$(printf '%s\\n' \"$created\" | jq -er '.result.workspace.worktree.is_linked_worktree')\ncreated_repo_key=$(printf '%s\\n' \"$created\" | jq -er '.result.workspace.worktree.repo_key')\ncreated_root=$(realpath \"$(git -C \"$checkout_path\" rev-parse --show-toplevel)\")\nsource_common_dir=$(realpath \"$(git -C \"$source_checkout\" rev-parse --path-format=absolute --git-common-dir)\")\ncreated_common_dir=$(realpath \"$(git -C \"$checkout_path\" rev-parse --path-format=absolute --git-common-dir)\")\n\ntest \"$created_linked\" = true\ntest \"$created_repo_key\" = \"$source_repo_key\"\ntest \"$created_root\" = \"$checkout_path\"\ntest \"$created_common_dir\" = \"$source_common_dir\"\n```\n\nAll compared paths are resolved physically with `realpath`. These are fail-closed checks before agent start: the result must be linked, have the source `repo_key`, name its own Git top-level as `checkout_path`, and share the source's absolute Git common directory. Do **not** require a linked checkout path to equal the primary source path. On any failure, report the response and any created workspace ID; do not continue in an unverified checkout.\n\nAfter verifying the created workspace, record the caller's control relationship locally, before starting the agent:\n\n```bash\nnode ~/.pi/agent/extensions/session-trail-footer/record-workspace.mjs \"$workspace_id\" \"$pane_id\"\n```\n\nThe helper uses the calling orchestrator's `HERDR_WORKSPACE_ID` / `HERDR_PANE_ID` as the parent and the verified creation response as the child. The repository's `source_workspace_id` is not an ownership parent. This records no Trail status and makes no remote request. On recording failure, preserve the created workspace, report the error, and stop before agent start. Retain this ownership-publication result in the launch receipt; it supports recursive local-only footers without requiring a parent to hand-write another CSV.\n\n## 3. Start and reach ready\n\nPass the selected native model only after `--`:\n\n```bash\nherdr agent start \"<agent-name>\" --kind \"<claude-or-pi>\" \\\n  --pane \"$pane_id\" --timeout 30000 -- --model \"<selected-model>\"\n```\n\nSuccess means ready. After a non-zero exit, inspect:\n\n```bash\nherdr agent get \"<agent-name>\"\nherdr agent read \"<agent-name>\" --source recent-unwrapped --lines 120\n```\n\nContinue only from confirmed `idle`. Wait if startup is `working`; escalate `blocked`; stop on persistent `unknown` or command failure. Never prompt merely because the process exists.\n\n## 4. Deliver with one bounded retry\n\nMake the **first** starter input a harness-native ownership-skill invocation carrying the complete supplied `starter_packet`; do not send a prose skill-loading preamble or a preliminary message:\n\n- Claude: `/owning-workspace-slice <starter_packet>`\n- Pi: `/skill:owning-workspace-slice <starter_packet>`\n\nConstruct `<starter-prompt>` by replacing `<starter_packet>` with the complete supplied packet, unchanged after the invocation and separating space. The launch still preserves normal skills, plugins, extensions, and project instructions through the invoked ownership skill.\n\nFrom confirmed `idle`, record the pre-prompt lifecycle state, set `prompt_attempts=1`, and submit without `--wait`. Retain the command response even on failure:\n\n```bash\npre_state=$(herdr agent get \"<agent-name>\")\nprompt_result=$(herdr agent prompt \"<agent-name>\" \"<starter-prompt>\" 2>&1)\nprompt_exit=$?\nprintf '%s\\n' \"$prompt_result\"\npost_state=$(herdr agent get \"<agent-name>\")\nherdr agent read \"<agent-name>\" --source recent-unwrapped --lines 120\n```\n\n| Observation | Decision |\n|---|---|\n| `working` | Confirmed; never resend. |\n| `done`, or `idle` with completion evidence | Record completion; never resend. |\n| `blocked` | Read and escalate; never resend. |\n| `unknown` | Recheck; never resend while ambiguous. |\n| Any ambiguous `idle` | Escalate; never resend. |\n\nThe sole retry is permitted only when **all** of the following are directly confirmed:\n\n1. `prompt_exit` is non-zero and `prompt_result` reports `agent_prompt_stalled`;\n2. `pre_state` and `post_state` are both confirmed `idle`, with no observed lifecycle/state advance between them; and\n3. `agent read` shows the untouched ready UI, with no task output or other evidence that the prompt was received.\n\nThen resend the identical prompt once and set `prompt_attempts=2`. A second genuine miss becomes `failed` with the prompt result and pane evidence; never inject a third time. `agent_prompt_stalled` plus observed `working` means delivery succeeded.\n\n## 5. Return the receipt\n\nReturn `source_workspace_id`, `workspace_id`, `pane_id`, `checkout_path`, `branch`, route receipt, `kind`, `model`, `agent_name`, `prompt_attempts`, ownership-skill name, and `final_state`. Focus only when requested.\n"}],"isError":false,"durationMs":81,"timestamp":1792375287038}}
{"type":"message","id":"01e35e92","parentId":"f85d624e","timestamp":"2026-10-19T02:01:27.038Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_c10cb465a0b94fbe9501d3a7aeedf9b4|fc_0b16ddefcb78f265016ad579f562bc81919ff389b234500fd0","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"---\nname: routing-workspace-agents\ndescription: Route curated skill model and harness selections, including workspace-agent dispatch waves, through the shared quota-aware authority.\n---\n\n# Route Workspace Agents\n\nThis router governs curated skills under `~/.claude/skills`, `~/.pi/agent/skills`, and `~/.agents/skills`. Any such skill that selects a model or harness must obtain a router receipt and honor its selected harness/model unchanged. Role-only or direct skills should describe capabilities without inventing local model maps, selected receipts, quota-based choices, or launch-time model selection. Run `python3 ~/.claude/skills/routing-workspace-agents/scripts/audit-skill-routing.py` to check the governed roots.\n\n`route.py` is the sole decision engine whenever a curated skill selects a model or harness. Callers classify the task and pass explicit operator constraints; they must pass the returned receipt unchanged and never synthesize or rewrite a selection. This skill never selects the top-level model or changes provider billing settings.\n\n## 1. Use the smallest suitable tier\n\n- `normal` (default): Claude `sonnet`; Pi `openai-codex/gpt-6-luna`\n- `high` (complex work, no approval needed): Claude `opus`; Pi `openai-codex/gpt-6-sol`\n- `premium` (explicit request only): Claude `fable`; Pi `openai-codex/gpt-6-astra`\n\nUse `normal` for settled, bounded work, including large but routine tasks. Choose\nor upgrade to `high` without asking when the outcome needs substantial architecture\nor tradeoff reasoning, an unsettled cross-piece contract, or a concrete reasoning/\ndesign blocker the normal tier cannot resolve. Record that rationale alongside\nthe routing receipt, without changing it.\n\nTask size, migration/security labels, multiple repositories, ordinary test failures,\nmissing context, or the current top-level model do not by themselves justify a\nhigher tier. A security-sensitive correction with a known contract and a settled\nfix, such as matching an exact Client ID instead of appending a suffix, is `normal`.\nRisk changes verification requirements, not automatically model size. Preserve the\nsame testing, review, and verification requirements regardless of tier.\n\nNever choose Astra or Fable automatically. You may suggest one when there is a\nconcrete blocker: explain what is stuck, what the smaller models tried, and why\nthat specific premium model may help. Warn that Fable is expensive. Ask and wait\nfor Alex's explicit request/approval for the named model before launching or\nswitching. A generic request to use a more capable model permits Opus/Sol, not\nAstra/Fable. Record the exact request or approval alongside the routing receipt.\n\nFor an authorized premium request, pass `--complexity premium --requested-model\nastra` or `--complexity premium --requested-model fable`. The helper requires the\nexact named model and never substitutes the other premium model. One-shot flags\n`--requested-model sonnet|opus|fable|astra` and\n`--requested-harness claude|pi` constrain the router's selection; incompatible\ncomplexity/model/harness combinations fail closed. Without a matching persistent\nprovider override or `--allow-extra-usage`, constrained choices require verified\nincluded capacity. `--allow-extra-usage` is valid only with a requested model or\nharness, records authorization in the receipt, and does not change billing or\noverride state. A matching persistent override retains its existing quota-bypass\nbehavior; a conflicting override fails without changing state.\n\n## 2. Manual overrides\n\nAlex can pin a provider from the CLI until cleared:\n\n```fish\nworkspace-router override codex\nworkspace-router override claude\nworkspace-router override clear\nworkspace-router status\n```\n\nInstall the command using the bootstrap instructions in `~/.claude/README.md`.\nState lives in `$XDG_STATE_HOME/workspace-router/override.json`, defaulting to\n`~/.local/state/workspace-router/override.json`. It is machine-local, not tracked.\n\nThe helper automatically reads this state. A manual override takes precedence over\nall quota eligibility and fallback rules, including exhausted or unavailable quota.\nIt preserves the model-tier mapping and adds `override: codex` or\n`override: claude` to the receipt, with an explicit manual-selection reason.\nPinning a provider is an explicit routing decision: paid usage may apply, but no\nbilling settings are changed and unknown Codex overflow balance is not inferred.\nDo not set or clear an override on Alex's behalf unless explicitly requested.\nInvalid or unreadable override state blocks routing; report the error instead of\nsilently treating it as auto. `override clear` removes invalid state too.\n\n## 3. Read cached quota once\n\nRun this once at the start of the dispatch wave; do not add `--force`:\n\n```bash\nlimits=$(openusage)\nprintf '%s\\n' \"$limits\"\n```\n\nFor each piece, pass the same JSON to the deterministic helper. `fallback-index` is the zero-based position in the wave, so unavailable evidence alternates Claude then Pi.\n\n```bash\nprintf '%s' \"$limits\" | python3 \"${CLAUDE_SKILL_DIR:-$HOME/.claude/skills}/routing-workspace-agents/scripts/route.py\" \\\n  --complexity normal --fallback-index 0\n```\n\nReturn the helper's `workspace-agent-route.v1` JSON unchanged as the routing receipt. One-shot callers pass their constraints using router flags; only the router may decide and issue the selected harness/model. For example:\n\n```bash\nprintf '%s' \"$limits\" | python3 \"$router\" --complexity high --fallback-index 0 \\\n  --requested-model opus\n```\n\nAuthorized `--allow-extra-usage` paths may omit the quota input; the receipt records unavailable quota evidence, the requested constraint, selection kind, and `allow_extra_usage: true`. The flag is invalid without a requested model or harness. This one-shot authorization never writes persistent override state.\n\n## 4. Honor the receipt\n\nWithout a manual override, the helper validates `openusage.limits.v1`, errors, timestamps, stale flags, provider data, included weekly capacity, and Claude's active-session capacity. A weekly reset at or before the snapshot's `generatedAt` is untrustworthy, just like stale or malformed evidence. A healthy provider is preferred when the other provider is untrustworthy.\n\nWhen both providers are healthy and eligible, the helper selects the higher score:\n`weekly remaining percentage / max(days until weekly reset, 1/24)`.\nDays are measured from the snapshot's `generatedAt`, not the wall clock; the\none-hour floor prevents a near-zero denominator. Equal scores prefer the earlier\nreset, then Claude. The existing `reason` string records both calculations and the\nselection explanation; no receipt fields are added. For example, Claude at 71%\nresetting in one day scores 71, beating Codex at 99% resetting in seven days\n(about 14.14). This is a deterministic quota-expiry preference, not a forecast.\n\n- `status: selected`: pass its `harness` and `model` unchanged to the workspace launcher, including when an `override` is present. Do not reject manual selections based on quota. For a premium model, the launcher also requires Alex's explicit request for that exact model; a receipt or provider override alone is not authorization.\n- `status: needs_extra_usage_decision`: do not launch automatically. Escalate the explicit paid-extra decision.\n- Both untrustworthy providers in auto mode: use the helper's alternation result and record that quota evidence was unavailable.\n\nClaude `extraUsage` is deliberately ignored. Do not rerun quota collection per piece or change a receipt to fit a preferred provider.\n"}],"isError":false,"durationMs":81,"timestamp":1792375287038}}
{"type":"message","id":"3944ccdf","parentId":"01e35e92","timestamp":"2026-10-19T02:01:27.038Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_b9b96fb3243c4a0ea57bf420a7b7f346|fc_0b16ddefcb78f265016ad579f562bc8191a95297c1921337fb","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"---\nname: orchestrating-work\ndescription: Use for explicit Herdr/delegation requests and automatically for multiple independently ownable outcomes, cross-repository ownership, or clearly long-running visible work.\ncompatibility: Herdr launches require HERDR_ENV=1, herdr CLI, and an existing primary repository workspace for each source repository.\n---\n\n# Orchestrate Work\n\nUse this skill only when its activation policy matches. Otherwise own an answer, investigation, review, or small coherent change directly.\n\n## Activation\n\nActivate immediately for an explicit request to orchestrate, parallelize, delegate high-level work, or use Herdr. Also activate when the request has multiple independently ownable outcomes, cross-repository ownership, or a clearly long-running visible piece.\n\nDo not activate solely because an investigation needs exploration, a task needs a second opinion, or a coherent change needs one bounded helper. A direct session that discovers a trigger loads this skill then.\n\n## Keep parent work shallow\n\nThe parent owns the requested product outcome, priorities, cross-piece contracts, Linear lifecycle, workspace routing, launch/delivery receipts, liveness, merge detection, post-merge verification, cleanup, and final cross-piece conclusion.\n\nExplore only enough to establish repository identity, independently shippable outcomes, dependencies, acceptance evidence, and Linear metadata. Never turn guessed files, symbols, architecture, or implementation steps into delegate requirements. When safe decomposition needs deep repository knowledge, launch one lead workspace agent to return an outcome and dependency map before splitting implementation work.\n\nFor every cross-repository source selection, validate the requested repository's canonical root and primary-workspace identity. UI focus and a guessed path are not evidence.\n\n## Drain to quiescence\n\nAn orchestration turn may settle only when no safe immediate action remains. Apply this on the initial turn and after every watcher, monitor, or subagent notification: a native completion wake resumes orchestration; it is input to the parent loop, not permission to stop with a status summary.\n\nBefore any final or progress response:\n\n1. reconcile the ledger, mission, and delivered events for every incomplete piece;\n2. consume completed outputs and attention requests;\n3. immediately perform every safe available action, including inspecting or nudging a worker, re-arming a watch, launching an unblocked dependency, refreshing required evidence, advancing post-merge verification, or cleaning an eligible workspace; and\n4. repeat until the requested outcome is complete or no safe immediate action remains.\n\nA progress-only response is not a stopping condition. Yield only when every incomplete piece is blocked solely on a recorded external or human signal, or is actively running with its parent-owned watch armed and exact next signal recorded. Name those revisit triggers when yielding. A worker's completed turn is never the parent outcome by itself.\n\n## Prepare the dispatch wave\n\nBefore each launch, the parent owns Linear mutations: correct team, project, milestone, labels, assignee, dependencies, and the transition to In Progress. The workspace agent may read its issue but must not mutate its lifecycle.\n\nBuild this six-field starter packet for every piece:\n\n1. desired outcome and why it matters;\n2. Linear issue ID and authoritative issue requirements;\n3. settled product decisions and hard constraints;\n4. cross-piece interfaces and dependencies;\n5. acceptance signals and granted authority;\n6. known evidence, separated from replaceable hypotheses.\n\nName `owning-workspace-slice` as the required workspace workflow. Do not add unverified repository implementation detail.\n\nInvoke `routing-workspace-agents` once per wave. Record each returned `workspace-agent-route.v1` receipt and pass its selected harness/model to `herdr-launch`. A `needs_extra_usage_decision` receipt is an escalation, never authorization to spend Claude extra usage.\n\nCreate a readable per-run ledger outside tracked repositories under `~/.local/state/herdr/orchestrations/`. When creating it, write this run header before the stream rows:\n\n```text\norchestration_id: <unique run ID>\nobjective: <one-sentence desired outcome>\norchestrator_workspace_id: <HERDR_WORKSPACE_ID>\norchestrator_pane_id: <HERDR_PANE_ID>\nstarted_at: <ISO-8601 timestamp>\n```\n\nThen keep one row per piece:\n\n```text\npiece_id, issue_id, source_workspace_id, workspace_id, checkout_path,\nbranch, harness, model, agent_name, pane_id, prompt_attempts,\nagent_watch_id, armed_at, pr_watch_id, post_merge_watch_id, cleanup_watch_id,\nstate, dependencies, pr, trail, cleanup, outcome\n```\n\nWhen the workspace owner reports its automatically created or linked Trail, record the full canonical `https://entire.io/<forge>/<owner>/<repo>/trails/<number>` URL in that piece's `trail` field immediately. This ledger supplies the recorded workspace ownership tree for the local-only open-Trail status lines in Pi and Claude, including nested controlled workspaces; do not substitute UI focus, `source_workspace_id`, checkout/repository similarity, or a global Trail scan. Do not bulk-evaluate ledger text as shell commands. Legacy ledgers without this header remain valid; never rewrite one merely to support a status report.\n\n### Publish local Trail visibility from existing watches\n\nThe footer never fetches Trail state, polls Entire, or starts a background refresh. When the owner, steward, or existing watch has already observed a Trail's actual `open`, `draft`, `merged`, or `closed` status, publish that fact locally:\n\n```bash\nnode ~/.pi/agent/extensions/session-trail-footer/record-state.mjs \"$trail_url\" \"$trail_status\" \"$observed_at\" --workspace \"$workspace_id\" --pane \"$pane_id\"\n```\n\nUse the Trail owner's verified workspace/pane IDs from its launch receipt, not the relaying orchestrator's IDs. In the owning workspace, omitting the two owner flags uses its actual Herdr caller context. Publication also records that workspace's own Trail association, but never infers a parent edge. Use a canonical URL and the ISO timestamp when the source was observed. A fresh direct observation may omit the timestamp; a relayed receipt must preserve it, never re-stamp stale evidence as current. Publish on initial linking and subsequent status changes observed by the existing workflow, including final Trail closure. Do not make another remote request or create another watch merely to update the footer. A failed lookup leaves local visibility unchanged. PR merge/approval/readiness and ledger cleanup states are not Trail status evidence. Keep ownership rows accurate; a status record alone never creates a parent-child relationship. Existing running agents must pick up this publication step at their next normal wake.\n\n## Launch and arm the workspace watch\n\nUse `herdr-launch` for every worktree launch. It alone validates the source root, creates the worktree, waits for readiness, delivers the starter packet, and performs the one permitted retry.\n\nIn Pi, activate the explicit orchestration guard before the first launch by calling `herdr_agent_monitor` with action `begin` and the absolute ledger path. The guard validates the pane-bound ledger and observes each successful local worktree creation. It blocks a later `herdr worktree create` until the prior created workspace has a matching valid watch receipt in `agent_watch_id` and `armed_at`. End the guard only after its final pending creation has a valid receipt.\n\nFor every `working` receipt, before any next launch:\n\n1. record the `workspace-agent-route.v1` and `herdr-launch` receipts, including source, route, prompt-attempt, and ownership data; ensure the launch's explicit caller-to-child control edge was recorded by `record-workspace.mjs` (repair a missing record from this orchestrator pane using the verified child `workspace_id` and `pane_id`, never the repository source as parent);\n2. create or update the persistent ledger row as `active`;\n3. arm the parent-owned harness watch and record its `agent_watch_id` and `armed_at`; then\n4. enter normal monitoring.\n\nDispatch is not complete until that watch receipt exists. If watch creation fails, preserve the already-running workspace, record the exact failure, stop launching additional pieces, and escalate. `blocked` and `failed` launch receipts escalate immediately and never enter monitoring.\n\n### Non-blocking prompt delivery\n\nUnder this skill, all `herdr agent prompt` delivery omits `--wait`: initial launcher delivery and its permitted retry, parent nudges, resumed work, steward reminders, and cleanup wake-ups. A delivery command is only a receipt that input reached the agent; it never settles agent work:\n\n```bash\nherdr agent prompt \"$agent_name\" \"$message\"\n```\n\nNever wrap that command in a synchronous or foreground Pi tool call, and do not attach a completion timeout to it. The parent must not turn an external Herdr workspace into a synchronous Pi tool call. Settlement belongs exclusively to the existing or newly armed harness-native watcher. After every nonterminal prompt, ensure the watcher is armed or re-armed before yielding; do not wait in the parent for the agent to become idle, done, or blocked.\n\n### Agent watch\n\nA watch observes liveness, dependency changes, completion, merge detection, and cleanup state. It is parent-owned, not process-existence polling or an agent self-watcher. It records each event in the ledger and re-arms while the workspace is active.\n\n- **Claude:** call `Monitor` with `description`, `command`, `persistent: true`, and bounded `timeout_ms`. Its command is exactly one event-driven `herdr agent wait \"$agent_name\" --until blocked --until done --until idle --timeout <bounded-ms>`; never use a polling loop. Record the task ID from `Monitor started (task <task-id>, persistent ...)` as `agent_watch_id`. On event or expected timeout, inspect the complete evidence below and create a fresh Monitor task if still active.\n- **Pi:** call `herdr_agent_monitor` with action `add`, the verified `agent_name`, and a bounded `timeoutMs`. Record its returned monitor ID and `armed_at` as `agent_watch_id` and `armed_at` before any next launch. The tool runs exactly one event-driven `herdr agent wait`, retains its bounded result, and wakes the parent natively. Do not create an async Pi subagent merely to run `herdr agent wait`. Do not replace the native monitor with a polling shell.\n\nOn every event or expected timeout, the parent runs `herdr agent get`, `herdr agent explain \"$agent_name\" --json --verbose`, and `herdr agent read \"$agent_name\" --source recent-unwrapped --lines 120`. Treat `blocked` or a visible blocker as `awaiting_input`; active child state labels as `child_active`; `working` with a parked foreground as `background_only`; and `screen_detection_skipped=true` with `idle` or `done` as `needs_inspection`. `idle` and `done` are terminal-looking signals, not completion without output evidence. A failed `agent get` or persistent `unknown` means the agent disappeared: preserve the workspace and its recorded cleanup recipe, then escalate. In Pi, only after the pane evidence proves completion, call `herdr_agent_monitor` with action `ack` and classification `complete`. The tool performs a fresh `get/explain/read`, requires both `state_change_seq` and `completion_seq`, and stores that bounded inspection receipt; an uninspected or incomplete exited monitor continues to block dispatch.\n\nNever re-arm against the unchanged current terminal state. Record `state_change_seq` and `completion_seq` from the inspection. Re-arm only after parent action or evidence of a new active transition, and record the fresh monitor ID. While Herdr lacks revision-keyed waits, a terminal state with `child_active` waits for a complementary `working|blocked` transition rather than another immediate `idle|done` match.\n\nWatch **creation failure** means no receipt was produced: preserve the already-running workspace, record the exact error, block further dispatch, and escalate. A later **runtime failure**—a Claude Monitor task failure or exit without a handled event, or a Pi monitor in `failed`/`stopped` state—has the same preservation, recording, dispatch-blocking, and escalation result; never remove automatically. These rules apply to agent, PR, post-merge, and cleanup watches. An expected timeout handled by inspection and a justified re-arm is not a runtime failure.\n\n## PR and review watch\n\nWhen a workspace reports a PR, record the receipt and transition `active -> review`; PR readiness is not merge confirmation or cleanup authorization. The workspace owner owns its review steward and all detailed review follow-up. The parent never launches a competing steward.\n\nFor human-facing parent reports (not artifact creation), verify a Trail match only from an actual link or association; repository or branch similarity is not evidence. When one or more verified matching Trails exist, show only their full clickable canonical Trail URL(s) and Trail state; do not identify or link the matched PR, including its URL, number, or identity. PR-derived evidence such as mergeability, check status, and unresolved-thread counts may remain in prose without identifying or linking the PR. If the Trail association exists but Trail state/detail is unavailable, show the Trail URL as unknown and do not fall back to the PR. A closed, merged, or unknown matching Trail remains primary; report its state or discrepancy rather than switching artifacts. If no matching Trail exists, show the clickable PR URL with `Trail: pending/not linked`. For multiple verified matching Trails, show all their canonical URLs and no PR. Never guess or manually create a Trail. Keep artifact creation, automatic Trail linking, review mechanics, personal gates, post-merge verification, and structured/internal PR evidence unchanged. Pi and Claude bottom status lines remain Trail-only.\n\nKeep the agent watch and add a parent-owned aggregate PR/CI/trail watch, recording `pr_watch_id`.\n\n- **Claude:** call `Monitor` with `description`, an aggregate readiness-transition `command`, `persistent: true`, and bounded `timeout_ms`. Record the task ID from `Monitor started (task <task-id>, persistent ...)` as `pr_watch_id`; after task exit or an event, refresh aggregate state and create a fresh task as required.\n- **Pi:** use `review_monitor` where applicable and re-arm it on every actionable transition.\n\nThe GitHub check named exactly `Partial Gates` is always an environment/testing artifact. Exclude it from watches and required-check summaries; it is never a wake signal and does not block merge readiness. This exclusion does not apply to `Entire Gates` or any differently named check.\n\nOnly monitors present in `.monitors[]` are configured. An absent monitor is `not configured`, does not block readiness, and does not create `insufficient_evidence`.\n\nThe parent reads only PR existence/current SHA, **current-head mergeability** (`MERGEABLE`, `UNKNOWN`, or `CONFLICTING`) and its next action/signal, required-check summary (excluding `Partial Gates`), Bugbot terminal presence for that SHA, unresolved GitHub-thread count, `entire trail finding list --freshness any` status/open-finding count, main-review receipt (Trail status/head, runner ID/state/outcome/evaluated SHA, Entire gate summary), **monitor receipt** (PR/Trail head, only monitor keys present in `.monitors[]` with values, states, evaluated SHAs, absent standard keys marked `not configured` without blocking or `insufficient_evidence`, per-canonical-classification disposition counts, evidence-recorded status, actionable count, insufficient-evidence count, and next signal), merge status, dependencies, and completed outcomes. Trail findings are authoritative; a green GitHub gate or opened PR alone is insufficient. Never read finding bodies or logs, monitor rationale, per-claim evidence, import repository-level implementation detail, prescribe fixes, or perform fixes.\n\n### Reconcile settlement before review nudges\n\nReconcile `trail-review=missing` against authenticated Trail detail before nudging or retriggering. Select from `.runners[]` by exact `runner_id == \"trail-review\"`; current-passed requires `state == \"evaluated\"`, `outcome == \"passed\"`, and `evaluated_at_sha == head_sha`, with the Trail head matching the current PR head. Record absent/not-configured, running, stale, failed, and current-passed distinctly. An empty auxiliary summary is never main-review evidence. Genuine absence is not a passing required review; report the configuration/evidence gap. If detail is unavailable, name the next authenticated observation rather than starting another review.\n\nCheck Trail status before mergeability: `.status == \"merged\"` may coexist with false `.mergeability.mergeable`; route direct merge confirmation to post-merge verification, not another review. For open Trails, `.mergeability.mergeable` and `.mergeability.gates[]` (checks, findings, base checks, approvals) are corroborating evidence only; Entire Ready-to-merge never substitutes for Alex's personal current-head gates below. When those gates hold, approval-only Entire failure means **automated work complete; human handoff**, not another review run or an automated approval-polling condition. Keep merge detection armed, but do not nudge the steward for approval.\n\nIf reconciled aggregate state contradicts the owner or stalls, nudge the workspace owner with only the discrepancy or counts and tell it to resume its steward. An absent receipt, or a stale or incomplete receipt for a monitor present in `.monitors[]` (including an unresolved insufficient-evidence count) is an aggregate discrepancy: nudge the owner to complete current-head monitor interrogation without fetching or reading rationale. A current-head `UNKNOWN` mergeability requires the owner to await GitHub's mergeability computation for that SHA; `CONFLICTING` requires the owner to resume its steward for the recorded rebase action. The owner/steward handles CI, Bugbot, threads, trail findings, monitor interrogation, verification, pushes, current-SHA Bugbot checks, re-fetched threads, and refreshed trail findings. Re-arm aggregate watching after each transition through merge. Pre-merge readiness requires stable aggregate zero unresolved review threads and zero open trail findings, with Bugbot terminal, current-passed main review, green required CI excluding `Partial Gates`, and `mergeable == \"MERGEABLE\"` on the current SHA, plus a current-head monitor receipt with no evaluating, stale, or failed present monitor (absent keys are `not configured`, do not block, and do not create `insufficient_evidence`), zero actionable claims, zero insufficient-evidence claims, and documented no-action dispositions, before reporting merge-ready. These pre-merge gates do not prevent recognizing an already merged piece by direct fetch.\n\nReport dependency changes and completed outcomes without importing repository-level implementation detail into the parent context. The parent alone makes the final cross-piece conclusion.\n\n## Post-merge verification and cleanup\n\nTrack each workspace through:\n\n```text\nactive -> review -> merge_confirmed -> post_merge_verifying -> cleaning -> removed\n```\n\nMerge readiness is not merge confirmation or cleanup authorization. After direct merge confirmation, record the actual merge commit, target default branch, and observed default-branch head, then enter `post_merge_verifying`. Bind a parent-owned event-driven build/check watch to that merge commit and default branch, not steward self-polling. Record `post_merge_watch_id`: Claude uses `Monitor` with `description`, a commit/branch-bound check-transition `command`, `persistent: true`, and bounded `timeout_ms`; Pi uses an async watcher. On event or expected timeout, directly refresh the commit-bound check summary and re-arm until terminal; never substitute a later default-branch head or the old PR head. If the default branch advances, retain the actual merge commit as the verification target.\n\nRequire an observed build/check set with the complete required-check inventory and terminal successful required checks for that actual merge commit/default branch; ignore `Partial Gates`. An empty check set is not success, nor are queued, running, missing, cancelled, or failed required checks. Record a current merge-commit success receipt: repository, default branch, actual merge SHA, observed branch head, check/run identities and URLs, terminal conclusions, and observation time. No cleanup, workspace removal, Linear Done, or overall piece Done before that receipt.\n\nOn failure, report the failed check and merge SHA, preserve the workspace in `post_merge_verifying`, and escalate to the owner for investigation. An apparently unrelated failure needs owner-held evidence and an explicit escalation to the parent/user, never silent exclusion or cleanup authorization; the next signal is a successful rerun on the same merge commit. Missing checks or watch failure also preserve the workspace and name the next signal/error. The steward remains pre-merge; the parent alone watches and accepts post-merge success.\n\nOnly after the success receipt, transition to `cleaning` and arm a parent-owned cleanup watch. For Claude, call `Monitor` with `description`, a repo-specific cleanup-transition `command`, `persistent: true`, and bounded `timeout_ms`; record the task ID from `Monitor started (task <task-id>, persistent ...)` as `cleanup_watch_id` and create a fresh task after event/expected timeout while cleaning. For Pi, record the async cleanup watcher run ID as `cleanup_watch_id`. It watches the final cleanup receipt through repository cleanup and `herdr worktree remove` success.\n\n1. wake the owner for its final repository-specific cleanup pass and collect its receipt;\n2. verify every recorded resource is stopped or intentionally retained;\n3. if the owner crashed, inspect and run only recorded cleanup commands one at a time;\n4. confirm the checkout is clean and unoccupied;\n5. run `herdr worktree remove --workspace \"$workspace_id\"` without `--force`;\n6. record cleanup and Linear receipts, transition to `removed`, then move the issue to Done.\n\nDirty, locked, occupied, cleanup-watch-failed, or cleanup-failed workspaces remain open and escalate with the exact remaining state. Never invent repository-specific teardown and never automatically force removal.\n"}],"isError":false,"durationMs":80,"timestamp":1792375287038}}
{"type":"message","id":"bb204cc3","parentId":"3944ccdf","timestamp":"2026-10-19T02:01:27.038Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_88222c85dfad4ec7b2b2eb12c3b3ab17|fc_0b16ddefcb78f265016ad579f562bc8191a8f57aa1d890a8fc","toolName":"bash","content":[{"type":"text","text":"HERDR_ENV=1 workspace=wPB pane=wPB:p1\n{\"id\":\"cli:workspace:get\",\"result\":{\"type\":\"workspace_info\",\"workspace\":{\"active_tab_id\":\"wGX:t1\",\"agent_status\":\"unknown\",\"focused\":false,\"label\":\"entire-brain\",\"number\":18,\"pane_count\":1,\"tab_count\":1,\"workspace_id\":\"wGX\",\"worktree\":{\"checkout_path\":\"/Users/alex/workspace/entire-brain\",\"is_linked_worktree\":false,\"repo_key\":\"/Users/alex/workspace/entire-brain/.git\",\"repo_name\":\"entire-brain\",\"repo_root\":\"/Users/alex/workspace/entire-brain\"}}}}\n[]\n?? .pi/subagents/\n4d0b06882ad19a22926d3ac668190c1b54ea15ff\n{\"runtime_guard\": \"PASS\", \"python\": \"3.14.8\", \"executable\": \"/opt/homebrew/bin/python3.14\", \"sha256\": \"1b5cb11d163e5ba34e2a05fc533c0ed89574d4425f9749a9d89405f651980e0d\", \"global_writes\": false}\n"}],"isError":false,"durationMs":1077,"timestamp":1792375287038}}
{"type":"message","id":"a981d843","parentId":"bb204cc3","timestamp":"2026-10-19T02:01:27.038Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_7cd1332ac22c4170b1117f54bcea5ffc|fc_0b16ddefcb78f265016ad579f562bc819189558dbd0a5ab3b7","toolName":"mcp","content":[{"type":"text","text":"{\"id\":\"COR-2164\",\"uuid\":\"0f8d2d0f-fcde-46e9-9b15-a59cbfbc049b\",\"title\":\"Assess inherited critical argv-parser advisory in benchmark Git tooling\",\"description\":\"Read-only dependency-update review found an existing baseline security concern, not a newly introduced version regression.\\n\\nParent independently checked immutable baseline ad65996bee631e3044773407df48edc1e85e0c17 and updated heads bfbb527633840fbd1e76f395810adb1e36eeffb3 (tar) / 9a3f538b386ad22dca0f9d7f896f4f9e9c9234f5 (npm group). All three resolve simple-git 3.36.0 and @simple-git/argv-parser 1.1.1 in scripts/bench/package-lock.json. The group updates node-llama-cpp 3.18.1 to 3.22.1, but these two resolved versions are unchanged.\\n\\nCurrent authoritative GitHub advisory GHSA-v5rq-49vh-5v5c is CRITICAL and not withdrawn; it affects npm @simple-git/argv-parser <2.0.1 and fixes at2.0.1. Its synopsis concerns VISUAL editor environment-variable omission from unsafe-editor detection. Lockfile presence is not proof that this project's usage reaches an exploitable operation. The read-only OSV lookup also returned GHSA-858h-whjf-mvg5, GHSA-g4wm-2vf7-vfgr and GHSA-x6jw-m9v5-85vh for simple-git3.36.0; verify exact status/ranges and relevant calls rather than treat counts as exploit evidence.\\n\\nBounded follow-up: trace actual benchmark/native-install simple-git operations and trusted versus untrusted editor/config/input flow, verify current authoritative advisory and upstream fix guidance, and determine a compatible official remediation. Do not force a simple-git4 major override or change unrelated dependencies without compatibility evidence. Record introduced-versus-inherited exposure explicitly; no production exploit or setup-go/macOS CGO causation is asserted. Passing CI alone is not security clearance.\\n\\nRelated update Trails:\\n[https://entire.io/gh/entireio/entire-brain/trails/269](<https://entire.io/gh/entireio/entire-brain/trails/269>)\\n[https://entire.io/gh/entireio/entire-brain/trails/272](<https://entire.io/gh/entireio/entire-brain/trails/272>)\\nAdvisory: [https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-v5rq-49vh-5v5c](<https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-v5rq-49vh-5v5c>)\\nNo implementation is started by this issue.\",\"projectMilestone\":{\"id\":\"b3b9c5e4-e502-4b63-9f45-382109d35556\",\"name\":\"Post launch\"},\"priority\":{\"value\":2,\"name\":\"High\"},\"url\":\"https://linear.app/entirehq/issue/COR-2164/assess-inherited-critical-argv-parser-advisory-in-benchmark-git\",\"gitBranchName\":\"alex/cor-2164-assess-inherited-critical-argv-parser-advisory-in-benchmark\",\"createdAt\":\"2026-10-16T02:58:29.297Z\",\"updatedAt\":\"2026-10-18T21:58:44.876Z\",\"archivedAt\":null,\"completedAt\":null,\"startedAt\":null,\"canceledAt\":null,\"dueDate\":null,\"slaStartedAt\":null,\"slaMediumRiskAt\":null,\"slaHighRiskAt\":null,\"slaBreachesAt\":null,\"status\":\"Backlog\",\"statusType\":\"backlog\",\"labels\":[\"Security\"],\"attachments\":[{\"id\":\"c49dd410-e8d0-4366-bd72-1ae38ecf2643\",\"title\":\"npm grouped dependency update — Trail 272\",\"subtitle\":\"Entire is a fast, distributed, Git-compatible network for mirroring GitHub repositories so agents can clone fast without hitting origin rate limits.\",\"url\":\"https://entire.io/gh/entireio/entire-brain/trails/272\"},{\"id\":\"1084ad53-30a3-401d-9cc1-dbbec9097018\",\"title\":\"Inherited npm Git-tooling advisory\",\"subtitle\":\"\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-v5rq-49vh-5v5c\"}],\"documents\":[],\"stateHistory\":[{\"state\":{\"id\":\"6170bf77-16d7-4d42-9fe6-e0090e8536a0\",\"name\":\"Backlog\",\"type\":\"backlog\"},\"startedAt\":\"2026-10-16T02:58:29.297Z\",\"endedAt\":\"2026-10-17T04:37:47.772Z\"},{\"state\":{\"id\":\"fe80236e-5498-407a-bfe8-a84e1cf6d25c\",\"name\":\"In Progress\",\"type\":\"started\"},\"startedAt\":\"2026-10-17T04:37:47.772Z\",\"endedAt\":\"2026-10-17T04:53:46.767Z\"},{\"state\":{\"id\":\"6170bf77-16d7-4d42-9fe6-e0090e8536a0\",\"name\":\"Backlog\",\"type\":\"backlog\"},\"startedAt\":\"2026-10-17T04:53:46.767Z\",\"endedAt\":\"2026-10-18T21:53:21.799Z\"},{\"state\":{\"id\":\"fe80236e-5498-407a-bfe8-a84e1cf6d25c\",\"name\":\"In Progress\",\"type\":\"started\"},\"startedAt\":\"2026-10-18T21:53:21.799Z\",\"endedAt\":\"2026-10-18T21:58:44.860Z\"},{\"state\":{\"id\":\"6170bf77-16d7-4d42-9fe6-e0090e8536a0\",\"name\":\"Backlog\",\"type\":\"backlog\"},\"startedAt\":\"2026-10-18T21:58:44.860Z\",\"endedAt\":null}],\"createdBy\":\"Alex Ong\",\"createdById\":\"45a00cf6-3a37-45ab-a508-d9da7f9d514c\",\"assignee\":\"Alex Ong\",\"assigneeId\":\"45a00cf6-3a37-45ab-a508-d9da7f9d514c\",\"project\":\"P0 · GA the Local Brain\",\"projectId\":\"91954c69-3fab-4281-84e0-cf4b81d85d75\",\"team\":\"Core\",\"teamId\":\"894690b6-2173-4cbe-abd3-6e7ea5ff5bb9\",\"relations\":{\"blocks\":[],\"blockedBy\":[],\"relatedTo\":[{\"id\":\"COR-2145\",\"title\":\"Assess setup-go v7 bundled XML-parser advisory and workflow exposure\"},{\"id\":\"COR-2154\",\"title\":\"Replace facts semantic retrieval (bundled embedder + RRF) with in-process BM25 + taxonomy filters\"},{\"id\":\"COR-2136\",\"title\":\"entire-brain: unblock nanoid security patch with isolated benchmark fixtures\"}],\"duplicateOf\":null}}"}],"details":{"mode":"call","mcpResult":{"content":[{"type":"text","text":"{\"id\":\"COR-2164\",\"uuid\":\"0f8d2d0f-fcde-46e9-9b15-a59cbfbc049b\",\"title\":\"Assess inherited critical argv-parser advisory in benchmark Git tooling\",\"description\":\"Read-only dependency-update review found an existing baseline security concern, not a newly introduced version regression.\\n\\nParent independently checked immutable baseline ad65996bee631e3044773407df48edc1e85e0c17 and updated heads bfbb527633840fbd1e76f395810adb1e36eeffb3 (tar) / 9a3f538b386ad22dca0f9d7f896f4f9e9c9234f5 (npm group). All three resolve simple-git 3.36.0 and @simple-git/argv-parser 1.1.1 in scripts/bench/package-lock.json. The group updates node-llama-cpp 3.18.1 to 3.22.1, but these two resolved versions are unchanged.\\n\\nCurrent authoritative GitHub advisory GHSA-v5rq-49vh-5v5c is CRITICAL and not withdrawn; it affects npm @simple-git/argv-parser <2.0.1 and fixes at2.0.1. Its synopsis concerns VISUAL editor environment-variable omission from unsafe-editor detection. Lockfile presence is not proof that this project's usage reaches an exploitable operation. The read-only OSV lookup also returned GHSA-858h-whjf-mvg5, GHSA-g4wm-2vf7-vfgr and GHSA-x6jw-m9v5-85vh for simple-git3.36.0; verify exact status/ranges and relevant calls rather than treat counts as exploit evidence.\\n\\nBounded follow-up: trace actual benchmark/native-install simple-git operations and trusted versus untrusted editor/config/input flow, verify current authoritative advisory and upstream fix guidance, and determine a compatible official remediation. Do not force a simple-git4 major override or change unrelated dependencies without compatibility evidence. Record introduced-versus-inherited exposure explicitly; no production exploit or setup-go/macOS CGO causation is asserted. Passing CI alone is not security clearance.\\n\\nRelated update Trails:\\n[https://entire.io/gh/entireio/entire-brain/trails/269](<https://entire.io/gh/entireio/entire-brain/trails/269>)\\n[https://entire.io/gh/entireio/entire-brain/trails/272](<https://entire.io/gh/entireio/entire-brain/trails/272>)\\nAdvisory: [https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-v5rq-49vh-5v5c](<https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-v5rq-49vh-5v5c>)\\nNo implementation is started by this issue.\",\"projectMilestone\":{\"id\":\"b3b9c5e4-e502-4b63-9f45-382109d35556\",\"name\":\"Post launch\"},\"priority\":{\"value\":2,\"name\":\"High\"},\"url\":\"https://linear.app/entirehq/issue/COR-2164/assess-inherited-critical-argv-parser-advisory-in-benchmark-git\",\"gitBranchName\":\"alex/cor-2164-assess-inherited-critical-argv-parser-advisory-in-benchmark\",\"createdAt\":\"2026-10-16T02:58:29.297Z\",\"updatedAt\":\"2026-10-18T21:58:44.876Z\",\"archivedAt\":null,\"completedAt\":null,\"startedAt\":null,\"canceledAt\":null,\"dueDate\":null,\"slaStartedAt\":null,\"slaMediumRiskAt\":null,\"slaHighRiskAt\":null,\"slaBreachesAt\":null,\"status\":\"Backlog\",\"statusType\":\"backlog\",\"labels\":[\"Security\"],\"attachments\":[{\"id\":\"c49dd410-e8d0-4366-bd72-1ae38ecf2643\",\"title\":\"npm grouped dependency update — Trail 272\",\"subtitle\":\"Entire is a fast, distributed, Git-compatible network for mirroring GitHub repositories so agents can clone fast without hitting origin rate limits.\",\"url\":\"https://entire.io/gh/entireio/entire-brain/trails/272\"},{\"id\":\"1084ad53-30a3-401d-9cc1-dbbec9097018\",\"title\":\"Inherited npm Git-tooling advisory\",\"subtitle\":\"\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-v5rq-49vh-5v5c\"}],\"documents\":[],\"stateHistory\":[{\"state\":{\"id\":\"6170bf77-16d7-4d42-9fe6-e0090e8536a0\",\"name\":\"Backlog\",\"type\":\"backlog\"},\"startedAt\":\"2026-10-16T02:58:29.297Z\",\"endedAt\":\"2026-10-17T04:37:47.772Z\"},{\"state\":{\"id\":\"fe80236e-5498-407a-bfe8-a84e1cf6d25c\",\"name\":\"In Progress\",\"type\":\"started\"},\"startedAt\":\"2026-10-17T04:37:47.772Z\",\"endedAt\":\"2026-10-17T04:53:46.767Z\"},{\"state\":{\"id\":\"6170bf77-16d7-4d42-9fe6-e0090e8536a0\",\"name\":\"Backlog\",\"type\":\"backlog\"},\"startedAt\":\"2026-10-17T04:53:46.767Z\",\"endedAt\":\"2026-10-18T21:53:21.799Z\"},{\"state\":{\"id\":\"fe80236e-5498-407a-bfe8-a84e1cf6d25c\",\"name\":\"In Progress\",\"type\":\"started\"},\"startedAt\":\"2026-10-18T21:53:21.799Z\",\"endedAt\":\"2026-10-18T21:58:44.860Z\"},{\"state\":{\"id\":\"6170bf77-16d7-4d42-9fe6-e0090e8536a0\",\"name\":\"Backlog\",\"type\":\"backlog\"},\"startedAt\":\"2026-10-18T21:58:44.860Z\",\"endedAt\":null}],\"createdBy\":\"Alex Ong\",\"createdById\":\"45a00cf6-3a37-45ab-a508-d9da7f9d514c\",\"assignee\":\"Alex Ong\",\"assigneeId\":\"45a00cf6-3a37-45ab-a508-d9da7f9d514c\",\"project\":\"P0 · GA the Local Brain\",\"projectId\":\"91954c69-3fab-4281-84e0-cf4b81d85d75\",\"team\":\"Core\",\"teamId\":\"894690b6-2173-4cbe-abd3-6e7ea5ff5bb9\",\"relations\":{\"blocks\":[],\"blockedBy\":[],\"relatedTo\":[{\"id\":\"COR-2145\",\"title\":\"Assess setup-go v7 bundled XML-parser advisory and workflow exposure\"},{\"id\":\"COR-2154\",\"title\":\"Replace facts semantic retrieval (bundled embedder + RRF) with in-process BM25 + taxonomy filters\"},{\"id\":\"COR-2136\",\"title\":\"entire-brain: unblock nanoid security patch with isolated benchmark fixtures\"}],\"duplicateOf\":null}}"}]},"server":"linear","tool":"get_issue"},"isError":false,"durationMs":1396,"timestamp":1792375287038}}
{"type":"message","id":"e23788cc","parentId":"a981d843","timestamp":"2026-10-19T02:01:27.038Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_1fdc2a78de0c41b4b4afcad0c233bad3|fc_0b16ddefcb78f265016ad579f562bc81919a87da033bb55c77","toolName":"mcp","content":[{"type":"text","text":"{\"id\":\"COR-2154\",\"status\":\"Backlog\",\"title\":\"Replace facts semantic retrieval (bundled embedder + RRF) with in-process BM25 + taxonomy filters\",\"description\":\"Drops the bundled embedder and RRF fusion for facts retrieval in favour of in-process BM25 plus taxonomy filters.\\n\\n**PR:** <pull-request id=\\\"141565d2-e97c-4b5f-9475-402f0b3009c7\\\" href=\\\"https://linear.app/entirehq/review/replace-facts-semantic-retrieval-bundled-embedder-rrf-with-in-process-b100a0100f20\\\">entireio/entire-brain#365</pull-request> — mergeable, Entire Gates red\\n\\n## This one does not ride the fix stack\\n\\nEvery other M2 PR is a truth-in-output fix with no behavioural risk. This changes **what facts come back**, so it needs its own review and its own measurement, and must not be folded into the integration branch.\\n\\nRequired before landing:\\n\\n* A retrieval quality comparison against the current ranker on a fixed query set, reported with the median beside the mean.\\n* Confirmation that dropping the bundled embedder does not change the install footprint in a way the release notes miss.\\n\\nPrior art on why a ranker change needs its own measurement: `semantic-fts5-trigram-refuted` (trigram looked right, measured 2.2× slower on real queries and 18× build).\",\"updatedAt\":\"2026-10-18T13:45:34.491Z\"}"}],"details":{"mode":"call","mcpResult":{"content":[{"type":"text","text":"{\"id\":\"COR-2154\",\"status\":\"Backlog\",\"title\":\"Replace facts semantic retrieval (bundled embedder + RRF) with in-process BM25 + taxonomy filters\",\"description\":\"Drops the bundled embedder and RRF fusion for facts retrieval in favour of in-process BM25 plus taxonomy filters.\\n\\n**PR:** <pull-request id=\\\"141565d2-e97c-4b5f-9475-402f0b3009c7\\\" href=\\\"https://linear.app/entirehq/review/replace-facts-semantic-retrieval-bundled-embedder-rrf-with-in-process-b100a0100f20\\\">entireio/entire-brain#365</pull-request> — mergeable, Entire Gates red\\n\\n## This one does not ride the fix stack\\n\\nEvery other M2 PR is a truth-in-output fix with no behavioural risk. This changes **what facts come back**, so it needs its own review and its own measurement, and must not be folded into the integration branch.\\n\\nRequired before landing:\\n\\n* A retrieval quality comparison against the current ranker on a fixed query set, reported with the median beside the mean.\\n* Confirmation that dropping the bundled embedder does not change the install footprint in a way the release notes miss.\\n\\nPrior art on why a ranker change needs its own measurement: `semantic-fts5-trigram-refuted` (trigram looked right, measured 2.2× slower on real queries and 18× build).\",\"updatedAt\":\"2026-10-18T13:45:34.491Z\"}"}]},"server":"linear","tool":"get_issue"},"isError":false,"durationMs":1315,"timestamp":1792375287038}}
